;;; Executable ADR: the public API a doeff subpackage (a package under packages/) shows its users is only effects, handlers and factories that return handlers,
;;; the types needed to build them, read their answers, catch their failures and compose handlers, and program helpers allowed name by name (functions that return
;;; a program that only emits effects — R18). Every other name is put inside, with an _ on the name or the module path, or remade into an effect, a handler or a factory. A handler is
;;; a callable value carrying the marker _doeff_is_handler_fn (the core ProgramHandler), and the official form of applying it to a program is h1(h2(p)).
;;;
;;; Provenance and how clauses are added: the rule was decided on 2026-10-26 (initiative doeff-subpackages-effects-only). Scope and boundary handling follow the answers to 6 questions and
;;; the follow-up questions asked again name by name (all 2026-10-27). The shape and application of handlers and factories follow the 2026-10-28 decision, which revised R3 and R6.
;;; R6 was also revised by the 2026-10-30 policy of not making scheduled a factory. R18 added program helpers. R19 made doeff-validation's check and perform
;;; types. R20 decided doeff-adr's get_adr, R21 adr_ids, and R22 the two Semgrep-check functions. R23 added doeff-vm-core and doeff-linter, which among the 7 tool packages
;;; not yet asked about are decided by clauses that already have answers. R24 made doeff-effect-analyzer's analysis functions effects and an analysis handler. R25 allowed, among the program
;;; functions of helper shape in the outlier groups, only the 155 names used from outside. R26 moved the analysis sample doeff-test-target from under packages to doeff-effect-analyzer's test
;;; dir. R27 decided the interface for querying doeff-indexer's index. R28 deleted the workflow's Rust CLI package and folded it into the Python CLI.
;;; R11, following the R10 answer, put doeff-vm outside the inventory's scope as an internal part of doeff. R29 decided doeff-domain's 8 functions that register, look up and check
;;; the table. R30 decided doeff-adr's 2 functions that verify ADR shape and the function that registers deftest checks.
;;; R31 decided where Hy macros outside doeff-hy live and the entry of doeff-hy's tools. R32 decided doeff-adr's 4 functions that read and write the registry.
;;; R33 put doeff-adr in the scope 'in rule scope' and deleted the submodule shim modules. R34 decided that no child with the same name as a name the entry shows
;;; (shim modules included) is placed (an import of an old path hid the entry's function behind a module, and the record service
;;; crashed on every startup on 2026-10-30 09:42-13:12 JST). R34 also applies to the entries of packages below the root (including _ packages).
;;; R35 allowed, name by name, functions converting an effect's answer to another measure (conversion functions — doeff-time's epoch_ms_of). R36 kept in the entry, as a startup call,
;;; the install that the .pth shipped by doeff-hy calls on every start (an extension of R10's plugin registration and R14, decided without asking). R37
;;; allowed builders that assemble values describing a program's shape and their types (such as doeff-stream's merge and subscribe, and the types they return).
;;; Things without an answer are listed under 'undecided' in R13, and when the answer comes the maintainer adds a clause to this ADR.
;;;
;;; Enforcement is held by a test (tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py). The inventory of the one place that counts the public API
;;; (tools/public_api_inventory.py) is matched against each package's known table (the violation table tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json — outliers and
;;; boundaries only, only shrinks) and the allowed table (tests/data/public_api_allowed.json — names the rule allowed name by name; may grow with a reason). The inventory tool calls names read as
;;; factories to check the marker, and counts names returning an unmarked value as outliers (R3). Names in the allowed table's group
;;; 'allowed — program helper' may be placed only if the inventory tool reads inside them and confirms the helper shape (R18). Names in the groups 'allowed — Hy
;;; macro' and 'allowed — CLI command' are also confirmed by the inventory row's form (R31) — the CLI command group may hold only commands a clause names.
;;; Names in the groups 'allowed — pytest plugin registration' and 'allowed — startup call' are confirmed by the inventory tool reading the pytest11
;;; entry points and the .pth of the package's pyproject.toml (R10, R33, R36). Names in the group 'allowed — conversion function' may be placed only if the inventory tool reads inside them and confirms the conversion shape (R35). Names in the group 'allowed — exception type'
;;; may be placed only if the inventory form is an exception class defined by that package (R2). Names in the group 'allowed — effect value type' may be placed only if they are types the inventory
;;; tool collected from the field annotations of the same package's effect classes (value_types) (R2). Names in the group 'allowed — program-shape
;;; builders and types' may be placed only if they are builders whose inventory form returns a class the package shows, or classes returned by the group's builders
;;; (R37). Names in the group 'allowed — handler setting type' may be placed only if they are types the inventory tool collected from the parameter annotations of the same package's handlers and factories
;;; (settings) and defined by that package (R2). This ADR makes no separate
;;; check — the laws' :enforced-by point at that test, and this file's deftests only call its functions (R12).
;;;
;;; Rollback: to drop the rule, delete this file, tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py, the known table and the allowed table, and regenerate the ledger (docs/adr/enforcement-ledger.json).
;;; To revise one scope or boundary clause, revise that clause and the known table's scope and groups in the same commit. Drop it only when there is a word from the maintainer.

(require doeff-adr.macros [defadr rule law])
(import doeff-adr.macros [fact interpretation counterexample])
(require doeff-hy.macros [defk deftest val <-])
(import importlib.util)
(import pathlib [Path])
(import sys)
(import tempfile)
(import types [ModuleType])


(val ROOT (get (. (.resolve (Path __file__)) parents) 2))
(val GUARD-TEST "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py")


(defk guard-module []
  {:pre [] :post [(: % ModuleType)] :tags {:context "doeff-api-adr" :role "foundation"}}
  "Load the enforcement test's module (tests/ is not a package — load it under a different name so it does not mix with what pytest collects).
   Put it in sys.modules before loading — dataclasses inside the module look up their own module in sys.modules when reading field annotations."
  (val spec (importlib.util.spec-from-file-location "_adr_doe_api_001_guard" (/ ROOT GUARD-TEST)))
  (val module (importlib.util.module-from-spec spec))
  (setv (get sys.modules spec.name) module)
  (.exec-module spec.loader module)
  module)


(defadr ADR-DOE-API-001
  :title "The public API a doeff subpackage shows its users is only effects, handlers and factories that return handlers, the types of their values, results, exceptions and settings, and program helpers allowed name by name (functions returning a program that only emits effects). A handler is a marked callable value (ProgramHandler), applied to a program as h1(h2(p)), not written as a class, and public names come only from the subpackage's entry. Enforcement is held by one test that matches the public API inventory against a known table that only shrinks; it calls factories to see a handler is returned, and reads inside helpers to see they neither install handlers nor answer effects"
  :status "accepted"
  :scope ["packages/*/ (the subpackage's import root, submodules, [project.scripts], entry points)"
          "packages/doeff-vm/ (an internal part of doeff — the inventory does not count it, and the table's test checks nothing outside doeff imports it, R11)"
          "tools/public_api_inventory.py (the one place that counts the public API)"
          "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py (enforcement)"
          "tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json (known table — the violation table, only shrinks)"
          "tests/data/public_api_allowed.json (allowed table — may grow with a reason)"
          "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy"]
  :problem
    [(fact
       "On 2026-10-26 it was decided that what a doeff subpackage gives its users is effects and handlers, and nothing else."
       :evidence "initiative doeff-subpackages-effects-only")
     (fact
       "Origin: on 2026-10-26, an agent package's tutorial and its video showed building its class ScenarioAgentHandler and wrapping a program with .wrap, and the root of that package exposed procedural functions such as launch_session and monitor_session that start and watch a session by hand. From that, users learn a usage that is not doeff's way of yielding effects and installing handlers."
       :evidence "the 69 names commit 548f04f62 removed from the root of that agent package, and its tutorial in the version before it")
     (fact
       "The public API inventory gave each public name of the 39 packages under packages/ a kind (effect, handler, other, unclassified), and split other and unclassified into outliers (with a fix direction) and boundaries (with a kind). What remained were boundaries for which the rule text alone does not decide in or out, and 12 tool packages that are not things exposing effects and handlers."
       :evidence "tools/public_api_inventory.py and the group names of tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json")
     (fact
       "The handling of boundaries and tool packages was asked as 6 questions (tool packages, types, class-shaped handlers, combinators, submodules, CLI commands and __version__), and all 6 were answered on 2026-10-27. Types and submodules were decided as proposed, and class-shaped handlers were decided in a direction outside the proposals (not written as classes). Tool packages, combinators, and CLI commands with __version__ were answered by asking again with each name shown concretely, and the follow-up questions decide them name by name."
       :evidence "answers to the 6 questions (2026-10-27)")
     (fact
       "Known violation table (main edc3802d5b5f): 13,753 rows across 39 packages and 31 rows of added names. Outlier groups and the direction to reduce them — outlier — move into the handler 2,498 (parts only the handler uses go into the _-prefixed part of the handler's module, R1), outlier — move to an inner module 2,276 (R1, R5), outlier — make it an effect 121 (R1)."
       :evidence "tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json (main edc3802d5b5f) — the source of truth for names is this table, and the numbers in this fact are a copy at the time of writing")
     (fact
       "Boundary groups and their handling in the known violation table (same version) — foundation and tool packages 6,653 (R10, R13), effect result types 857, effect value types 834, exception types 214, handler setting types 115 (allowed by R2, R12), workflow DSL words 66 (the workflow package's dsl, R13), class-shaped handlers 55 (R4), combinators 23 (R6, R13), names readable as handlers (the tool cannot read them) 19 (give the handler a return type so the inventory reads it as a handler), unsorted 16 (R13), CLI 4 (kept by R7, R12), Hy macros 2 (doeff-docker's with_handlers and doeff-events' event_loop — R13)."
       :evidence "tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json (main edc3802d5b5f)")
     (fact
       "Packages with many rows in the known violation table (same version) — doeff-cluster 5,192 (awaiting a scope answer), doeff-records 1,507, doeff-core-effects 1,273, doeff-hy 735 (awaiting a scope answer), and 2,985 in three agent and workflow packages since removed from this repository. Fixes proceed from the packages in rule scope with the most rows, per phase of the initiative doeff-subpackages-effects-only."
       :evidence "tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json (main edc3802d5b5f)")
     (fact
       "Public submodules (R5 outliers) are 731 rows across the 39 packages in the known table (410 in the 28 packages in rule scope, 321 in the 11 packages awaiting a scope answer). The children directly below the import roots in rule scope number 267, and renaming a child to _<name> hides the modules below it too. The fix was split into 31 cards across stages 1-3."
       :evidence "the (submodule) rows of tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json (inventory of doeff ec6311c0a), artifact 2026-10-28-doeff-submodule-hide-split.html (733 rows and 265 first-level children at doeff 37467d478138 — an agent package and doeff-hy-pytest then reduced submodules)")
     (fact
       "On 2026-10-28 it was decided that: a handler is a marked callable value (the core ProgramHandler) and the official form of applying it to a program is h1(h2(p)); WithHandler is the core's raw form and does not appear in user-facing docs; a one-off handler is written with Hy's handle macro, and a reused handler is named as a defhandler or a factory returning a handler; doeff-core-effects' maybe, result, on_raise, absent_as and scheduled are treated as factories returning handlers, like the core state(). What a subpackage may show became effects, handlers and factories returning handlers, and a factory is confirmed structurally (by the marker) to return a handler when called."
       :evidence "the 2026-10-28 decision")
     (fact
       "On 2026-10-30 a policy was set to avoid the factory form for handlers where possible. scheduled, which the 2026-10-28 decision made a factory, got on 2026-10-29 the factory form scheduled(), a shim layer keeping the old form scheduled(p), and a tool rewriting callers on doeff's main; under this policy the caller rewrite was abandoned and the rewrites in doeff, agora-controllers and moneta were reverted. scheduled has neither settings nor per-call state, so like maybe and result in ADR-DOE-API-002 R1 it is not made a factory."
       :evidence "the 2026-10-30 policy, and the revert of the rewrites in doeff, agora-controllers and moneta")
     (fact
       "The inventory (tree of doeff 19e34510931f, 2026-10-29) counted 225 definitions across 39 packages (435 public rows) readable, by return type or return shape, as factories returning handlers. Called with minimal arguments, 202 (69 with no arguments, 133 with placeholders) returned a marked handler or a set of handlers, 21 raised when called (ones that read the placeholder when called, a tmux_handler looking for tmux on a machine without tmux, and so on), and 2 — the workflow package's make_scheduled_handler and make_offloaded_scheduled_handler — returned an unmarked (effect, k) @do function (a handler body). maybe, result, on_raise, absent_as and scheduled have a program (DoExpr, Program) as return type, so they are not read as factories; called with no arguments they lack a program, and given a program they return an unmarked program."
       :evidence "the reasons on inventory rows of tools/public_api_inventory.py (at the end of a factory row, how it was called and what came back), the group 'outlier — reshape as a factory returning a handler' of tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json, and the 6 rows of added names added by the factory check (R3)")
     (fact
       "On 2026-10-29 it was decided that program helpers that are neither effects nor handlers — functions returning a program that only emits effects — may be shown, and that doeff-traverse's sequence is shown as is. The question was whether to hide or allow sequence (the hiding direction would rewrite 5 places in doeff-cluster into for loops)."
       :evidence "the answer to the question whether to hide or allow sequence (2026-10-29)")
     (fact
       "The inventory (the tool with the R18 judgment added, on the tree of doeff c344561a81e5, 2026-10-29) read, of 3,538 public rows of functions returning a program, 3,441 rows (2,539 names) as helper shape (installs no handler inside and answers no effect), 93 rows as installing handlers, and 4 rows as answering effects. Most helper-shape rows are in the known table's outlier groups — move to an inner module 2,163, move into the handler 1,024, make it an effect 91."
       :evidence "the forms and reasons of inventory rows of tools/public_api_inventory.py (at the end of the reason of a row going beyond a helper, which function reads which name) and the groups of tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json")
     (fact
       "The inventory (tree of doeff 6675fb4c72c1, the make sync environment, 2026-10-29) counted the public rows of the 7 tool packages not yet asked about — doeff-vm-core 0 (no Python module), doeff-linter 1 (a Rust executable), doeff-indexer 1 (console script), the workflow's Rust CLI package 1 (a Rust executable), doeff-effect-analyzer 64 (6 submodules, 1 console script, 57 names), doeff-domain 99 (5 submodules, 94 names), doeff-test-target 180 (29 submodules, 151 names). doeff-indexer and doeff-effect-analyzer are not installed in this environment, and the inventory reads them from the source text — Indexer and SymbolInfo, which doeff-indexer's root exports from its Rust extension (read by doeff/cli/discovery.py in doeff's root), are not counted."
       :evidence "inventory rows of tools/public_api_inventory.py --package <dir>, packages/doeff-indexer/python/doeff_indexer/__init__.py, packages/doeff-indexer/src/python_api.rs")
     (fact
       "Recounting the helper-shape rows of the outlier groups with the inventory of doeff 9c0707dd87a2 gives 3,264 rows and 2,460 names (names are the number of definitions). Of these, only 155 names (271 rows) are imported by the origin/main of the 12 outside repos (agora-controllers, merge-queue, moneta and others) or another doeff package — by group, move to an inner module 100, move into the handler 49, make it an effect 6; by repo, agora-controllers 131, merge-queue 23, moneta 9 and others. The 2,305 unused names are moved under _ by the hiding cards of phase 5."
       :evidence "count record (table 1, table 2, the list of 155 names), artifact 2026-10-29-r13d-helper-counts.html")
     (fact
       "On 2026-10-30 it was decided to allow doeff-time's epoch_ms_of (a function flooring a time with a zone to an integer of milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC) name by name as a conversion function, without changing its callers. epoch_ms_of is the single owner of the rounding formula (when 2 places stamp times with rounding and flooring respectively, a later stamp can appear earlier — #3855); its callers are 5 files in doeff (doeff-cluster and an agent package) and 2 files in agora-controllers (315 files and 973 lines through the shared conversion epoch-ms-of-time), and the other 19 repos have none. A handler changing the rounding cannot exist, and making it an effect would only mean the conversion can be written only under a clock handler, with no benefit from swapping."
       :evidence "the answer to the question whether to allow epoch_ms_of (2026-10-30), the count of callers (origin/main of the 21 checkouts in ~/repos and doeff's main, 2026-10-30 01:00 JST)")
     (fact
       "On 2026-10-30 it was decided to add a clause allowing builders that assemble a program's shape, checked by the inventory. The question was whether the workflow package's DSL words (66 rows and 47 names in the known table — 19 types such as AgentSpec, and 28 builders such as agent, gate and loop) should be shown as value types and builders for writing workflows, remade into effects, or moved inside so that only macros (defworkflow and others) write them. A builder only takes values and returns a value of a type of the same package (the workflow's shape — AgentSpec, LoopSpec and others); effects are made when the type's value is expanded (AgentSpec.to_effect). The macros agent!, gate!, loop and others call builders in their expansion. The inventory of doeff 789275644022 read all 28 as returning a value type and reading neither effects nor I/O inside (conversion functions)."
       :evidence "the answer to the question on handling the workflow DSL words (2026-10-30), the workflow package's _dsl.py and macros.hy")]
  :context
    [(interpretation
       "doeff's value is that the same program runs in tests and in production by swapping handlers. When a subpackage shows procedural functions or client classes, users call those interfaces directly and write outside of handler swapping. If only effects and handlers are shown, every subpackage is used just by yielding effects and installing handlers.")
     (interpretation
       "A handler is a callable value carrying the marker _doeff_is_handler_fn (the core ProgramHandler — it takes a program and returns the wrapped program). Its content is a body taking (effect, k) that either answers (Resume) or passes outward (Pass), and @handler, defhandler and stacked_handlers attach the marker (ADR-DOE-CORE-003). A function that returns a handler or a set of handlers when called, like the core state(), is a factory returning a handler. Even a function taking a program and returning a program is not a handler if unmarked, and one that returns an unmarked value when called is not a factory — what is counted is the marker, not a reading of the shape.")
     (interpretation
       "When a handler is a value, applying it to a program is just a call (h1(h2(p)) — wrap from the outside in the order h1, h2), and naming it for reuse or passing it around is the same as for any ordinary value. The core WithHandler(handler, program) is the raw form passed to the VM, not a form users write.")
     (interpretation
       "Types are part of effects and handlers: without value types effects cannot be built, without result types answers cannot be read, without exception types failures cannot be caught, and without setting types handlers cannot be built.")
     (interpretation
       "How public names are counted is held in one place, the inventory tool: names the import root's __init__ exports, names exported by submodules no part of whose path starts with _, and those submodules themselves (R5), [project.scripts], entry points and maturin bins. This ADR holds no separate counting. doeff at the repo root (the core of program, do, run, handler) is not a subpackage and the inventory does not count it. The same goes for internal parts of doeff imported only by doeff (doeff-vm — R11).")]
  :decision
    [(rule R1 "A subpackage's public API is only effects (EffectBase types and functions that build effects), handlers (marked values from @handler, defhandler, stacked_handlers), factories returning handlers (functions that return a handler or a set of handlers when called — R3), program helpers allowed name by name (R18) and conversion functions (R35) (the 2026-10-26 rule, written out up to factories by the 2026-10-28 decision and up to helpers by the 2026-10-29 answer). Other names (procedural functions, program functions not allowed by R18, client and API classes, functions reading settings, constants) are put inside, with an _ on the name or the module path, or remade into an effect, a handler or a factory.")
     (rule R2 "Effect value types, effect result types, exception types raised by effects and handlers, and handler setting types may be public as part of effects and handlers (2026-10-27). Exception types are put in the allowed table's group 'allowed — exception type' with an answer id, and the inventory tool confirms the form of the name — only names whose inventory form is an exception class (a subclass of BaseException) defined in a module under that package's dir or under its import root may be placed. Names re-exporting an exception of another package or the standard library, and classes that are not exceptions, are red when placed (2026-10-30 — the 247 rows, 16 packages and 129 names of the known table's group 'boundary — exception type' were moved). Effect value types are put in the allowed table's group 'allowed — effect value type' with an answer id, and the inventory tool confirms the form of the name — only classes and type aliases that appear, including type arguments (list[X], X | None), in the field annotations (dataclass fields, or parameters of an __init__ written in Python) of the effect classes the same package shows, collected also by following the fields of the value types the package defines (the inventory's value_types — the Y in a field of the X in an effect's field is also a value type), may be placed. A class that appears in no effect's field is red when placed. Value types whose field annotation does not state a type, such as object, a bare tuple or dict, or Any, are invisible to the inventory, so they are put in the known table's group 'outlier — type the effect field or move it inside', and moved to the allowed table after the field is typed — moved inside if not an effect value (2026-10-30 — of the 757 rows and 278 names of the known table's group 'boundary — effect value type', 95 names and 298 rows plus 25 rows of added names were moved; the 25 names and 107 rows appearing in effects' answer types (__doeff_answer__) were moved to the group 'boundary — effect result type', and the remaining 158 names and 352 rows to that outlier group). Handler setting types are put in the allowed table's group 'allowed — handler setting type' with an answer id, and the inventory tool confirms the form of the name — only classes and type aliases defined by that package that appear, including type arguments, in the parameter annotations of the handlers, handler classes (__init__) and factories the same package shows may be placed. The types of fields of setting types the package defines are followed too (the inventory's settings — e.g. the type PingLevel of a field of the factory parameter PingSettings). Types appearing in no parameter, functions, and names re-exporting a type of the standard library or another package are red when placed. Types of parameters without a type, such as a Hy defn parameter or backend=None, are invisible to the inventory, so they are put in the known table's group 'outlier — type the factory parameter or move it inside', and moved to the allowed table after the parameter is typed — moved inside if the type is not passed to a handler (2026-10-30 — of the 179 rows and 109 names of the known table's group 'boundary — handler setting type', 64 names and 112 rows plus the 3 rows of the added name ScenarioWorld were moved; the remaining 44 type names and 66 rows went to that outlier group, and the function default_session_backend to the group 'outlier — move to an inner module').")
     (rule R3 "A handler is a callable value carrying the marker _doeff_is_handler_fn (the core ProgramHandler), whose content is a body taking (effect, k) that answers or passes outward (the 2026-10-28 decision). A factory returning a handler is a function that, called with no arguments or the minimal arguments, returns that value (or a list or tuple of such values). An unmarked function is not counted as a handler even if it takes a program and returns a program, and a function that returns an unmarked value when called is not counted as a factory even if its return type says ProgramHandler. The inventory tool calls names read as factories with minimal arguments (an empty placeholder for each parameter without a default) to check the marker, and counts names returning an unmarked value as outliers (functions not returning a handler). Factories that raise when called (ones that read the placeholder when called, ones that look for a command missing on the machine) stay as read from the return type. The form before the revision: the 2026-10-27 'a handler is limited to a function that takes (effect, k) and answers; a function that only takes a program and returns a program is not counted as a handler' — decision (1) made a handler a ProgramHandler value, so counting was revised from the shape (program → program) to the marker. That an unmarked program → program function is not counted as a handler is unchanged.")
     (rule R4 "Handlers are not written as classes. A class-shaped handler whose name ends with Handler and that receives effects in a method is not hidden inside but rewritten as a doeff handler (@handler, defhandler) and the class deleted (2026-10-27). The fixes are per-package cards (doeff-git and the workflow packages).")
     (rule R5 "Public names come only from the subpackage's import root (__init__). Submodules are hidden with an _ on the name, and submodules not starting with _ are counted as outliers (2026-10-27). The fixes were split per subpackage.")
     (rule R6 "Functions wrapping a program (combinators) are decided name by name (2026-10-27). doeff-core-effects' on_raise and absent_as are treated as factories returning handlers, like the core state() (the 2026-10-28 decision) — the direction is the form on_raise(catch…)(p): calling returns a handler and the program is passed to that handler. on_raise and absent_as were reshaped as factories (2026-10-29 — calling returns a marked handler, and doeff-hy's on-raise and absent-as expand to this form too). maybe and result have neither settings nor per-call state, so they are marked handler values, not factories, applied as maybe(p) and result(p) (2026-10-29 — ADR-DOE-API-002 R1; revised from the factory maybe()(p) of the 2026-10-28 decision. The old calls maybe() and result() emit a DeprecationWarning for one version and return the same handler — ADR-DOE-API-002 R3). The 4 old forms maybe(p), result(p), on_raise(p, catch…) and absent_as(default, p) were deleted after the callers in repos reading doeff's main were rewritten (2026-10-29 — old forms are refused with TypeError, and the 4 names were removed from the known table's group 'outlier — reshape as a factory returning a handler'). scheduled stays a handler applied directly to a program: having neither settings nor per-call state it is not made a factory, but is a handler value carrying the marker _doeff_is_handler_fn, applied as scheduled(p) (scheduled(p, implementation=…) to choose the implementation) (the 2026-10-30 policy that handlers avoid being factories where possible — the same treatment as maybe and result in ADR-DOE-API-002 R1). Revised from the factory scheduled()(p) of the 2026-10-28 decision: the factory form scheduled(), the shim layer and the caller-rewriting tool added on 2026-10-29 were deleted, and scheduled() becomes a TypeError for a missing program. The 2 rows of scheduled are read as handlers by the marker, so they were removed from the known table's group 'outlier — reshape as a factory returning a handler', and the group became empty and was removed. The 8 names that installed handlers over a program (16 rows of boundary groups in the known table) were brought to the factory or handler-value form by the 2026-10-30 decision (direction decided by R3 and R16): the workflow package's make_blocking_scheduled_handler and make_async_scheduled_handler (take fn and return a handler answering every effect with fn(effect)) and doeff-notify's testing_handler and build_testing_handler (take the capture destination NotificationCapture) became factories under their names; doeff-core-effects' run_apart has neither settings nor per-call state, so it became a marked handler value under its name rather than a factory (run_apart(p) — ADR-DOE-API-002 R1); the agentic workflow package's with_agentic_effectful_handlers(p, …) was folded into the existing factory agentic_effectful_handlers(…)(p), and with_handlers(p, handlers) (its runtime submodule) into doeff's stacked_handlers(*handlers)(p), deleting the names and the submodule. doeff-records' ready_handlers is not a function installing handlers but a program preparing the check shell (returns writer name → handler as is), so it was not made a factory but moved inside as _ready_handlers. Callers of all 8 names in outside repos reading doeff's main were 0, so they were changed without a shim layer. The form before the revision: the 2026-10-27 'maybe and result are, for now, an exception to the rule allowed as public functions' — decision (3) made the 5 factories, so the exception was removed and they moved to the factory side. writer_log was remade into the effect ReadWriterLog, which reads the log, and a handler. Of the remaining 6, closing_links, sequence, try_call and validate are allowed as R18 program helpers, and check and perform were made the types Check and Perform and the functions deleted (R19).")
     (rule R7 "CLI commands people type (such as doeff-flow) are kept, and their content only runs programs emitting the subpackage's effects under handlers. Client and API classes for CLI commands are put inside the subpackage (2026-10-27). The fixes were split into 3 cards.")
     (rule R8 "Code does not start processes through CLI-command subcommands. Resident processes are split into per-role Python module entries, starting them is an effect, and the handler starts them with sys.executable -m <module> (not looking at PATH). The agent package's CLI command doeff-sessionhost was deleted (2026-10-27).")
     (rule R9 "Subpackages do not have __version__. The version is read with importlib.metadata.version (2026-10-27).")
     (rule R10 "The 12 tool packages that are not things exposing effects and handlers are neither put in scope nor left out together, but decided per package (2026-10-27). Decided — doeff-vm: the user's entry is doeff's root, and only doeff's inside imports doeff_vm. Types for writing handlers missing from the root are added to the root, and the 9 functions measuring and configuring counts become effects and handlers. doeff-vm's scope is R11 (outside the inventory's scope as an internal part of doeff). doeff-hy's macros: the group writing effects, handlers and programs (defeffect, defhandler, defk, <- and 14 others), deftest, and the foundation group (defrecord, val, var, deff and 10 others) are shown. The group that turns things into values is aligned: absent-as and on-raise with R6's factories, check and validate with the R13 answer. conductor's 13 workflow macros move to the workflow package and defsystem to doeff-cluster. doeff-hy-pytest: shown names are narrowed to 3 — the pytest11 plugin registration and 2 per-test declarations (declare_step_budget, declare_process_tree_cpu). Classes and functions inside the measurement move inside, and outside repos are changed to read the summary lines the plugin emits (the maintainer cuts the fix cards). doeff-cluster is R15. doeff-vm-core and doeff-linter are R23. doeff-effect-analyzer is R24. doeff-test-target is R26 (moved outside packages). doeff-indexer is R27. The workflow's Rust CLI package was deleted by R28. doeff-domain is R29. The last package, doeff-adr, is R33 (removed from R13 (b)).")
     (rule R11 "The scope is every package under packages/ except internal parts of doeff. An internal part of doeff is a package imported only by doeff's root package (doeff/), currently only doeff-vm (the R10 answer — users read VM types from doeff's root, and doeff's subpackages read VM internals from doeff._vm). A part is not a subpackage users import, so like doeff's root the inventory tool does not count it (DOEFF_PARTS in tools/public_api_inventory.py) and the known table does not list it (2026-10-30 — the 64 doeff-vm rows (1 submodule, 63 names) of the table's scope 'scope awaiting answer' and group 'boundary — foundation and tool packages' were removed). Instead of counting, the same table's test checks that files outside doeff (packages under packages/ other than the parts, examples, Hy under docs/adr) do not import a part's import root — reading import, from, Hy's import and require, and name strings passed to importlib.import_module or pytest.importorskip (law a-doeff-part-is-imported-only-by-doeff). doeff's root package and doeff's checks (tests/) are inside doeff and may read the VM directly. The repo's benchmarks (tools measuring the VM), the doeff-publications repo (published articles, moved out of doeff on 2026-11-04) and design evidence in docs (past records) are not counted either. Repos outside doeff are invisible to doeff's checks, so their rewrite (8 repos and 69 places in the 2026-10-30 count — agora-controllers 50) and per-repo watching are held by another card. doeff-vm's content, the Rust crate doeff-vm-core, has no Python module and stays 'in rule scope' as per R23. It now applies to the 36 packages of the known table's scope 'in rule scope'. Packages 'scope awaiting answer' are now 0, and the last one, doeff-adr, was moved to 'in rule scope' by the R13 (b) answer as per R33 (when adding a package without an answer, revise the table's scope and groups to the answer once it comes — the maintainer cuts the revising card). Moved to rule scope by answers: doeff-hy by R14, doeff-cluster by R15, doeff-vm-core and doeff-linter by R23, doeff-effect-analyzer by R24, doeff-indexer by R27, doeff-domain by R29, doeff-adr by R33. doeff-hy-pytest is 'in rule scope' as per the R10 answer. Removed from under packages: doeff-test-target was moved by R26, the workflow's Rust CLI package deleted by R28. The form before the revision: the 2026-10-27 'the scope is every package under packages/. The 12 packages awaiting a scope answer get the table's scope and groups revised to the answer, starting with those R10 answered (doeff-vm, doeff-hy, doeff-hy-pytest)' — the R10 answer decided only doeff's inside imports doeff_vm, so doeff-vm is not put in scope but placed outside it as an internal part of doeff.")
     (rule R12 "Enforcement is in one place: tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py matches the inventory of tools/public_api_inventory.py per package against 2 tables — the known table (the violation table tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json — outliers and boundaries only) and the allowed table (tests/data/public_api_allowed.json — names the rule allowed name by name). An outlier in neither table is red; a name in a table but not in the inventory is red (either table); the known table's 'names' groups only shrink. Outliers that can only be added are written one row each with a reason in the known table's added names. Each row of the allowed table has a group and a decision (the clause allowing that row, or for rows without a clause, one English sentence of reason), and rows may be added with a reason — rows without a reason, and rows with the same name as in the known table, are red. The known table holds no allowed groups (red when placed), and can become empty once outliers and boundaries are all fixed (decided 2026-10-30 — the form that literally meets phase 6's clause 'the table is empty'; before, allowed things were also put in known-table groups). R3's factory check (call and look at the marker) is also inside the inventory tool, and names returning an unmarked value when called become outliers by the same matching. R18's helper check (reading inside) is also held by the inventory tool, and the same test turns red if a name in the allowed table's group 'allowed — program helper' is not of helper form. Only groups whose form the inventory checks may be put in the allowed table — currently 12: 'allowed — program helper' (R18), 'allowed — Hy macro', 'allowed — CLI command' (R31), 'allowed — conversion function' (R35), 'allowed — ADR-writing functions' (R14), 'allowed — effect answer type', 'allowed — exception type', 'allowed — effect value type', 'allowed — handler setting type' (R2), 'allowed — pytest plugin registration' (R10, R33), 'allowed — startup call' (R36), 'allowed — program-shape builders and types' (R37). The group 'allowed — CLI command' may hold only commands this ADR's clauses name and decide to keep (the test's ADR_COMMANDS — pairs of command and clause; the same test also checks that the clause text names it. Adding a command not in the ADR is red). When the inventory cannot read a package in rule scope with a Rust extension by import (it read it from the text), the same test turns red too — reading the text does not count names the entry exports from the extension (R27). This ADR makes no separate check. Things allowed by answers, like R2's types and R7's CLI commands, also stay in the known table's boundary groups until an allowing group whose form the inventory tool checks is added, and are not hidden or fixed — the card adding the allowing group moves those rows to the allowed table with a reason.")
     (rule R13 "Undecided (when an answer comes, the maintainer adds a clause to this ADR and cuts a card revising the known table's scope and groups): (a) none — doeff-validation's check and perform, the remaining combinators, were decided by R19 (closing_links, sequence, try_call and validate by R18). (b) none — doeff-adr, the last tool package, was decided by R33 (the 2026-10-30 answer — put it in scope; get_adr by R20, adr_ids by R21, register_semgrep_enforcement and assert_semgrep_enforcement by R22, assert_adr_contract, assert_all_adr_contracts and register_deftest_enforcement by R30, register_adr, clear_registry, enforcement_ids and get_enforcement by R32). The other tool packages have answers too (doeff-vm placed outside the scope as an internal part of doeff by R11, doeff-cluster by R15, doeff-hy-pytest by R10, doeff-vm-core and doeff-linter by R23, doeff-effect-analyzer by R24, doeff-test-target by R26, doeff-indexer by R27, doeff-domain by R29; the workflow's Rust CLI package deleted by R28). (c) none — the workflow package's DSL words (the 66 rows of its _dsl module) were decided by R37 (2026-10-30). The 16 unsorted rows (where Hy macros outside doeff-hy live, doeff-hy's CLI doeff-hy-check and the pytest plugin module) were decided by R31. (d) none — the program functions of helper shape in the outlier groups (3,278 rows in the 2026-10-29 inventory — move to an inner module 2,163, move into the handler 1,024, make it an effect 91) were decided as R25 by the 2026-10-29 answer (allow only used names): the 155 names used by outside repos or another doeff package are allowed name by name by R18, and the 2,305 unused names move inside as per R1 and R5.")
     (rule R14 "doeff-hy is put in the scope 'in rule scope' (2026-10-29 — names other than the macros of the R10 answer were decided as per R1, R2, R5). Macros, as per R10's groups, are shown from the 3 submodules that export macros (doeff_hy.macros, doeff_hy.record, doeff_hy.handle) — Hy's require reads macros by module path, so these 3 are put at the boundary outside R5. Names other than macros follow R1, R2, R5: R2 types may be exported from the entry, the rest move to inner modules, and the remaining 50 submodules under doeff_hy and doeff_hy_bytecode_guard are hidden with an _ on the name. After hiding, names added to a submodule are not public by default. The fixes were split into child cards. The 36 non-macro names the 3 macro modules exported (43 rows in the known table — imports of expansion parts, the constant HELPERS_NAME, the class RuntimeDo, and require_check called by defrecord's :check expansion) were moved inside (2026-10-30): imports were bound under _ aliases, HELPERS_NAME and RuntimeDo got an _ on the name, and require_check moved to the inner Python module doeff_hy._record_check (the expansion calls doeff_hy._record_check.require_check). What outside repos read from the 3 module paths was only macros, and files reading the moved names were 0. The inventory names of the 3 modules are now only Hy macros.")
     (rule R15 "doeff-cluster is put in the scope 'in rule scope' (2026-10-29 — the answer was hide). Names follow R1, R2, R5: only names outside repos use (count of the document — 423 of 3,443 public names) are shown from the entry, and among them the names to make effects and to move into handlers are fixed by later cards. Names unused outside move to inner modules. The entry is placed at the import root doeff_cluster as per R5 — the current doeff_cluster is a Hy namespace package without a root __init__, so the former move tool creates a lazy-import entry at the root and moves the sections directly below the root (coordinator, foundation, record_store, shared, sim, worker) to _<section>, hiding the submodules below (217 in the 2026-10-29 inventory) at once. The question document's 'code after choosing' showed reading from the section path (doeff_cluster.sim), but R5, the inventory tool and the table's test count names exported from the import root, so users' imports become (import doeff_cluster [sim-cluster StartWorker]). The macro defsystem (R10) is shown from the public module doeff_cluster.macros directly below the root, put at the boundary outside R5 — because Hy's require reads macros by module path (the same reason as R14). Step 1 of the shared section hid the body inside the section (doeff_cluster._shared.entry.macros), and doeff_cluster.macros re-exports defsystem (the allowed table's group 'allowed — Hy macro'). After hiding, names added to a submodule are not public by default. The fixes were split into child cards (step 1 per section, step 2 in user repos, step 3 deleting shim modules and table rows).")
     (rule R16 "The official form of applying a handler to a program is h1(h2(p)) — calling the handler value to wrap (the 2026-10-28 decision). The core WithHandler(handler, program) is the raw form passed to the VM and does not appear in user-facing docs and examples. The maintainer cuts the card rewriting WithHandler in the current docs.")
     (rule R17 "A handler applied once on the spot is written with Hy's handle macro (listing clauses per effect, like match, and applying them on the spot — doeff_hy.handle), and a reused handler is exported named as a defhandler or a factory returning a handler (the 2026-10-28 decision). What a subpackage shows is the named one (R1).")
     (rule R18 "Functions returning a program that only emits effects (program helpers) may be shown (2026-10-29). A function counted as a helper meets these 3: (1) returns a program — an @do or defk function, or a function whose return type is a program (Program, DoExpr and others). (2) does not itself take and answer effects — reads no handler control inside (Resume, ResumeThrow, Transfer, TransferThrow, Pass). A function taking (effect, k) is an R3 handler, not a helper. (3) installs no handler — reads inside neither WithHandler nor with_handlers, nor handler values, builders (handler, stacked_handlers) or factories returning handlers. The inside is the function together with everything reached by following the same package's functions it reads (catching and re-raising exceptions inside a called program is not answering an effect, so it stays a helper). A function whose inside cannot be read (not a Python function) is not counted as a helper. The inventory's reading looks at the names inside as global values or attribute names and follows only the same package's functions, so the form applying a handler received as an argument, the form applying a handler read from a module container or a class or instance attribute, and the form where another package's function installs it are invisible and read as helpers. Hence helpers are allowed name by name, and people read the inside of allowed names. Helpers are placed name by name in the allowed table (tests/data/public_api_allowed.json) with the group 'allowed — program helper' and a reason (helpers shown later are added the same way with a reason; before, they were put in a known-table group and in the 'group' field of added names), and the inventory tool confirms the form of the name and turns red if it is not of helper form (R12). Even if of helper form, a program function not in the allowed table remains an outlier (R25). Names allowed now: doeff-traverse's sequence and try_call (as per the answer), doeff-core-effects' closing_links and doeff-validation's validate of the same form (handled under the same clause), and the 155 names used by outside repos or another doeff package (R25). check and perform do not return a program, so they are not helpers, and R19 made them types.")
     (rule R19 "doeff-validation's check and perform are made types, not functions: the check spec is the type Check (formerly CheckSpec) and the effect-execution mark is the type Perform (formerly Performed), and users write the types directly as Check(predicate, args…, reason=…) and Perform(program) (2026-10-29 — make them types). Checking that the predicate is callable and that a Perform's value is a program or an effect, and building the expression's text, are held by the types' construction (either failing gives TypeError at construction), and the functions check and perform were removed from public names. The 2 are counted as R2 types as value types of validate's items, and as per R12 stay in the known table's added names until the inventory tool counts R2 types as allowed. Hy users write the check macro, whose expansion also builds the type Check (each argument is passed as a CheckArgument with a thunk delaying evaluation into the item, and Check uses it as is).")
     (rule R20 "doeff-adr's get_adr (a function looking up an ADR in the registry by id) became the effect GetAdr and the factory adr_registry returning a handler that answers from the registry (2026-10-29 — make it an effect). The body looking up the registry was put inside as _get_adr. The public function get_adr was, in the same order as R6, kept as a shim layer giving the same answer as the new form, and deleted after the callers in repos reading doeff's main were rewritten to GetAdr (agora-controllers raised its doeff pin in the same change) — the 2 rows in the known table (doeff_adr and doeff_adr._registry) were removed too (2026-10-29 — as the structural review found, the users of the name to delete were fixed first). adr_registry() reads the process's registry when answering, and adr_registry(specs=[AdrSpec …]) answers only from the given list (an id not in the list raises KeyError instead of falling through to the outer registry). The one installed inner answers first, so a test can swap in a different table under the outer default handler. What is installed under deftest, keeping the rule that doeff-adr does not choose handler sets (doeff-adr's README), is the project's doeff_interpreter fixture — for doeff, docs/adr/conftest.py installs adr_registry(). Callers in outside repos (agora-controllers, agora, custody) were rewritten by another card.")
     (rule R21 "doeff-adr's adr_ids (a function listing the ids of the ADRs in the registry) became the effect AdrIds, answered by the same handler of R20's factory adr_registry (2026-10-29 — make it an effect). adr_registry() lists the process registry's ids when answering, and adr_registry(specs=[AdrSpec …]) lists only the ids of the given list (it reads the same table as GetAdr). The listing body was put inside as _adr_ids. The public function adr_ids, in the same order as R20's get_adr (fix the users of the name to delete first), was first kept as a shim layer giving the same answer as AdrIds (doeff L3079), and after the callers in repos reading doeff's main (one function each in agora's docs/adr/defadr_0007_governance_charter.hy and custody's docs/adr/defadr_0008_auth_custody.hy — agora L6159, custody L74; agora-controllers has no caller) were rewritten to AdrIds, deleted in the same card, along with the 2 rows in the known table (doeff_adr and doeff_adr._registry).")
     (rule R22 "doeff-adr's register_semgrep_enforcement (a function registering a Semgrep check in the table) and assert_semgrep_enforcement (a function running semgrep and checking the samples' hits and misses) became the effects RegisterSemgrep and AssertSemgrep and the factory semgrep_enforcements returning a handler answering both (2026-10-29 — make both effects). The bodies moved inside as registry._register_semgrep_enforcement and registry._assert_semgrep_enforcement. The 2 public functions, in the same order as R20 (fix the users of the name to delete first — the structural review's finding), were first kept as a shim layer giving the same answer as the new form (doeff 4bd260a69), and after the direct callers in repos reading doeff's main (agora-controllers' scripts/semgrep_fixture_tree.hy and controllers/{kanban,durable}/tests/test_semgrep_fixtures.hy — agora-controllers does not pin doeff but reads the sibling main, so deleting first would turn the 3 files into import errors and stop the landing checks of every branch) were rewritten to the effects (agora-controllers cef8e6e88a; agora and custody have no callers), the 2 functions were deleted from registry and the entry, along with the 4 rows in the known table (2 names each of doeff_adr and doeff_adr._registry). The only interfaces for registering and verifying Semgrep checks are the effects RegisterSemgrep and AssertSemgrep, and the bodies _register_semgrep_enforcement and _assert_semgrep_enforcement remain inside. The handler is kept separate from R20's adr_registry: AssertSemgrep starts an outside process (semgrep), so it is not mixed into the handler set that only reads ADRs. The semgrep process is started when this handler answers AssertSemgrep. semgrep_enforcements() registers in and reads the process registry, semgrep_enforcements(table={…}) registers in and reads the given dict (an id not in the dict raises KeyError instead of falling through to the outer registry), and semgrep_enforcements(semgrep=…) starts semgrep with the given executable (str or Path; AssertionError if missing). Defined failures of registration and checking (AssertionError, KeyError, ValueError) are thrown back with TransferThrow to where the effect was emitted. The one installed inner answers first, so a test can run part of a program with a different table or a different semgrep. The macro defsemgrep's expansion runs registration at import time and the check inside the generated test, running these effects under this handler — it does not rely on the project's doeff_interpreter fixture, so defsemgrep in outside repos works just by raising the doeff version.")
     (rule R23 "doeff-vm-core and doeff-linter are put in the scope 'in rule scope' (2026-10-29, decided by the answered clauses R1 and R7 — decided without asking). doeff-vm-core is a Rust crate (the content of doeff-vm) without a module importable from Python, with 0 public names — nothing to fix, and once in scope R1 and R5 apply from when a Python surface is added. doeff-linter has no Python module, and its only public name is the Rust executable doeff-linter. It is a command people type and the commit hook calls, so it is kept as per R7 and put in the known table's group 'boundary — CLI' (R12). R7's 'their content only runs programs emitting the subpackage's effects under handlers' is the clause preventing a CLI command from becoming a separate interface that bypasses the subpackage's effects and handlers to read and write state. doeff-linter is a static checking tool with neither effects nor handlers, so there is nothing to bypass, and there is no fix rewriting the Rust executable into a doeff program (the workflow's Rust CLI package read directly from Rust the records answered by the agentic workflow package's effects and handlers, so the same reading did not decide it and it was left in R13 (b) — later deleted by R28). R7's question document had, as a premise, deferred the CLI commands of the 12 tool packages to another document, so R7's answer text does not decide doeff-linter — R23 relies on the request naming R7 among the answered clauses, is an extension decided by R7's purpose, and the maintainer can overturn it.")
     (rule R24 "doeff-effect-analyzer is put in the scope 'in rule scope', and its analysis functions were made effects and an analysis handler (2026-10-29 — make them effects; show the answer types). The entry doeff_effect_analyzer exports only the effects AnalyzeProgram (effects emitted by a Program function → ProgramEffects), AnalyzeHandler (effects a handler answers and effects its clauses emit → HandlerEffects), AnalyzeEnv (the handler list of an env factory) and CheckCoverage (whether env and the handlers a Program installs itself answer every effect → Coverage), the factory effect_analysis returning a handler answering the 4, and R2 types (the answer types ProgramEffects, HandlerEffects, Coverage, Gap, Basis and the value types of CheckCoverage's fields SuppliedDeclaration, FoldCarried). effect_analysis(result_cache=True) reads from disk CheckCoverage's answer for the Program function read there while the contents of the files of the modules it depends on are unchanged (the role of the former function cached_result — importable_name, which looks up the key's name, was also moved into the handler). The carrier counting carried Programs where they are emitted is passed as the value type FoldCarried (declared, names) instead of the former function runs_where_performed and the caller's predicate (include). Names not worth making effects were moved inside: qualified_name (a type's import name — callers spell it with __module__ and __qualname__), module_ast (read from inside by ADR-DOE-ANALYZER-001's check), observe_expansions (the process-wide hook watching Hy expansions — not something requested inside a program, so not made an effect; only doeff-hy-pytest's plugin inside doeff reads it from the inner _program_effects). R18 is the clause on functions returning programs, so it does not apply to these 3. The 6 submodules (cli, env_places, handler_effects, hy_expansion, program_effects, result_cache) were hidden with an _ as per R5, and the 50 names unused outside were put inside (the Rust core's analyze and analyze_symbol were also removed from the entry and remain in _native). The CLI command doeff-effects is kept as per R7, and its content runs the same effects under effect_analysis (_cli.main). The old paths program_effects and handler_effects, in the same order as R20 (fix the users of the name to delete first), were first kept as a shim layer for only the 5 names outside repos import (analyze_program, check_coverage, qualified_name, runs_where_performed, ProgramEffects) (doeff 4579b86aa), and after the callers in outside repos reading doeff's main (3 check files in agora-controllers — agora-controllers 6edd72897 — and 1 check file in merge-queue — merge-queue 1a0a489a, which raised its doeff pin to 4579b86aa) were rewritten to the effects AnalyzeProgram and CheckCoverage and FoldCarried, the 2 modules were deleted, along with the known table's outlier groups (make it an effect, hide the submodule with _, move to an inner module). Callers inside doeff (doeff-cluster's foundation-closure, 3 check files in doeff-events and doeff-records, the CLI) were rewritten to the effects — foundation-closure installs the analysis handler itself, so its callers add no handler. The inventory tool puts the source dirs of maturin packages last on sys.path and counts uninstalled pure-Python entries by importing them rather than from the text (an installed build, if present, comes first).")
     (rule R25 "Of the program functions of helper shape in the outlier groups, only names used by outside repos or another doeff package are allowed name by name by R18, and unused names move inside as per R1 and R5 (2026-10-29 — allow only used names). The used names are the 155 names counted with the inventory of doeff 9c0707dd87a2 and the origin/main of the 12 outside repos (number of definitions; 271 table rows — doeff-cluster 62, doeff-records 39, doeff-core-effects 29, doeff-hy 8, doeff-events 6, doeff-gemini 2, doeff-openai, doeff-openrouter and doeff-system-one 1 each, and 6 in agent packages since removed from this repository), moved from the known table's groups 'outlier — move to an inner module' 181 rows, 'outlier — move into the handler' 75 rows and 'outlier — make it an effect' 15 rows to the group 'allowed — program helper' (these 271 rows were then moved with reasons to the allowed table tests/data/public_api_allowed.json — R12). Rows where another module re-exports the same definition were moved as the same name. The source of truth for names is the allowed table. As per R18 the inventory tool confirms the form of this group's names, and the table's test turns red if one stops being of helper form (R12). doeff-cluster's 62 names fall under R15's 'names outside repos use' and are shown from the entry. the former move tool exports to the entry names whose group is not an outlier and names in the allowed table, so names in submodules not yet hidden are exported to the entry by the hiding card's hide. The 4 pairs with two different definitions of the same name (doeff-records' metric_word, doeff-cluster's stopped, kube_headers, file_sha256) are reported by the tool as a 'collision' without adding them to the entry, so the hiding card renames one or picks one. The 36 names of children already moved under _ in step 1 (8 of doeff-hy's wire, json_lines and http, doeff-records 11, doeff-core-effects 8, doeff-events 4, and 5 in agent packages since removed from this repository) were left under _ as outliers by step 1's hide, so the entry does not export them — they are re-exported from the entry before step 3 deletes the shim modules (the maintainer cuts the card; if step 3 deletes first, the allowed table's rows disappear too, and the names to re-export are added back to the allowed table with reasons). Outside repos still read these 36 names from the old paths (the shim modules), and files reading them from the _ paths are 0, so no rewrite returning outside repos' imports to the entry is needed — step 2's rewrite points names the entry exports at the entry. The 2,305 unused names remain in the outlier groups, and phase 5's hiding cards move them under _ and step 3 removes the table rows — no fix cards for making effects or moving into handlers are needed (R1 is also met by putting them inside). The 50 unused names exported directly from entries were removed. Helpers outside repos start using later are added name by name to the allowed table with the group 'allowed — program helper' and a reason (R18).")
     (rule R26 "doeff-test-target is not a subpackage distributed to users but doeff-effect-analyzer's analysis sample, so it was moved from under packages to doeff-effect-analyzer's test dir packages/doeff-effect-analyzer/tests/fixtures/doeff_test_target (2026-10-29 — move it outside). The sample's content was not changed. Its readers are the Rust check tests/effect_tracking.rs (copies the sample to a temporary dir and analyzes it) and 3 check files confirming the sample works on current doeff (test_fixture_package_* in tests/python — the running helper is fixture_package_run_support). The sample is not installed as a package; the checks put tests/fixtures on sys.path to read it. pytest does not collect under tests/fixtures (collect_ignore in tests/conftest.py — the sample's effects/test_effects.py is a module name, not a check). It is not under packages, so the inventory tool does not count it, and the known table's rows (29 submodule rows and 133 name rows of the scope 'scope awaiting answer' and group 'boundary — foundation and tool packages') were removed from the table.")
     (rule R27 "The interface for querying doeff-indexer's index became the effect FindSymbols (module path, marker word, kind → the answer is a tuple of SymbolInfo) and the handler rust_indexer answering with the Rust indexer (2026-10-29 — make it an effect). The former interface, the class Indexer (Indexer.for_module(m).find_symbols(…)), and the extension's class SymbolInfo were moved into the inner extension doeff_indexer._native and are not exported from the entry. The answer row type SymbolInfo is shown from the entry as an R2 effect result type, as a Python frozen dataclass (the handler copies the extension's answer — a test can build SymbolInfo in its own handler and answer FindSymbols). As per R12 it stays in the known table's added names until the inventory tool counts R2 types as allowed. rust_indexer has neither settings nor per-call state, so it is a handler value, not a factory (ADR-DOE-API-002 R1). The effect and answer types (doeff_indexer._effects) do not read the extension; only the handler (doeff_indexer._handlers) reads it — effects can be emitted and answered by a test handler without the extension's .so. A failure to build the index throws RuntimeError or OSError back to where FindSymbols was emitted, and doeff run's discovery catches it inside the program (discover), shows a warning and searches nothing (falls back to the default interpreter). If doeff-indexer is not installed, discovery silently does not search; if it is installed but the extension cannot be read, it warns (the structural review's finding). The Python interface changed, so the version is 0.2.0. The CLI command doeff-indexer is kept as an R7 CLI people type (its content is the bundled Rust binary — IDE plugins call it) and put in the known table's group 'boundary — CLI'. The callers were doeff run's discovery (doeff/cli/discovery.py — doeff run runs the program discover emitting FindSymbols under rust_indexer) and docs-video's discover.py, both rewritten. When the inventory reads a package with a Rust extension from the text it does not count names exported from the extension, so doeff-indexer was put in the root's dev group to read it by import, and the table's test turns red if a package in rule scope with a Rust extension cannot be read by import (R12). doeff-indexer was moved to the scope 'in rule scope'.")
     (rule R28 "The workflow's Rust CLI package (answering workflow listing, monitoring and attaching) is deleted, and its executable is folded into the Python CLI of the agentic workflow package (2026-10-29 — delete and fold). The Python CLI (a console script of the same name) answers the same 6 verbs as the deleted CLI (ps, show, watch, attach, send, stop) with the same arguments and JSON, and its content is a program handling records and tmux with that package's effects and handlers (R7 — leaving no interface that bypasses handlers to read records directly). The 2 IDE plugins (VS Code's doeff-runner, neovim's doeff.nvim) still call the same executable name, and their install instructions were changed to install the Python package. With the package deleted, its rows were removed from the known table (the (executable) row of scope 'scope awaiting answer' and group 'boundary — foundation and tool packages'), and it was removed from R13 (b). The agentic workflow package was later moved out of this repository, so its console script is no longer in the tables.")
     (rule R29 "doeff-domain is put in the scope 'in rule scope', and its 8 functions that register in, look up and check the table (register_domain, isolated_registry, get_domain, handles, handled_effects, assert_domain_covered, assert_registered_domains_covered, assert_no_orphan_effects) were made the effects RegisterDomain, GetDomain, HandledEffects, AssertDomainCovered, AssertRegisteredDomainsCovered, AssertNoOrphanEffects and the factory domain_registry returning a handler answering from the table (2026-10-29 — make them effects, ADR-DOE-DOMAIN-001 R8). The callers were only doeff's ADR-DOE-DOMAIN-001 and the package's tests, so the functions were deleted without a shim layer and the bodies put inside as doeff_domain._registry, _checks and _introspect. domain_registry() reads the process table (where the macro defdomain registers at import time) when answering, and domain_registry(domains=[Domain …]) answers from a separate table of only the given domains — replacing isolated_registry; RegisterDomain also registers into that table, and a name not in the table raises KeyError instead of falling through to the outer table. The separate table is created once when the factory is called, and RegisterDomain's registrations persist while the same handler value is reused (call the factory again if each test needs a new table). Overlapping domain names and a second introduction raise when the factory is called, not inside the program. Check failures are subclasses of DomainError — modules that cannot be imported during the scan are also wrapped in DomainCheckError (the original exception is __cause__) — and a name not in the table is KeyError. Both are thrown back with TransferThrow to where the effect was emitted (the structural review's finding narrowed failure types to 2). handles (an annotation on a handler) is neither a factory returning a marked handler when called (R3) nor a helper returning a program that only emits effects (R18) — handles(Ask) returned an unmarked function and was a declaration retrofitting a marker onto other packages' handlers at import time. An import-time declaration cannot be an effect emitted inside a program, so as with R19 it was made a type: Handles(handler, effect …) is put in a domain's handlers, so the declaration lives on the domain side and attaches nothing to the handler. Handles is counted as an R2 type as a value of a field of Domain, the value of the effect RegisterDomain, and as per R12 stays in the known table's added names until the inventory counts R2 types as allowed. The declaration types Domain, DomainLaw, DomainTerm (R2 effect value types) and 8 exceptions (R2) are shown from the entry, and the macro defdomain from doeff_domain.macros — Hy's require reads macros by module path, so for the same reason as R14 and R15 it is put at the boundary outside R5. Names unused outside (clear_registry, domain_names, introducing_domain, registered_domains, the constants BODY_ATTRIBUTE and HANDLES_ATTRIBUTE) and the 4 submodules (checks, introspect, registry, core_effects_domains) were moved inside with an _ as per R1 and R5.")
     (rule R30 "doeff-adr's assert_adr_contract (a function verifying the shape of one ADR), assert_all_adr_contracts (a function verifying the shape of every registered ADR) and register_deftest_enforcement (a function registering a deftest check in the table) became the effects AssertAdrContract, AssertAllAdrContracts and RegisterDeftest, answered by the same handler of R20's factory adr_registry (2026-10-30 — fix doeff-adr's remaining names in the same direction as R20-R22 and hide them inside). Verification reads the same ADR table and check table as GetAdr and starts no outside process, so it goes in adr_registry rather than R22's semgrep_enforcements. adr_registry gained the parameter enforcements= (a dict of checks): if neither specs nor enforcements is given it reads the process registry when answering; if either is given it is a separate registry, reading ADRs only from the specs list and checks only from the enforcements dict (the one not given is empty — the empty dict when enforcements is not given is created when the factory is called, and RegisterDeftest registers into it). Verification misses (accepted but no check, a named check not in the table, an expected-red check not tied to a plan) are AssertionError, an ADR id not in the table is KeyError, and overlapping registration ids and unknown modes are ValueError, all thrown back with TransferThrow to where the effect was emitted. AssertAllAdrContracts verifies in id order and throws the first miss. The bodies were put inside as registry._assert_adr_contract (takes the ADR value and the check table) and registry._register_deftest_enforcement (takes the check table). Callers in outside repos were 0 (the 2026-10-30 count, the default branches of every repo in ~/repos — the words appear only in a dotfiles check comment and ADR text), so as with R29 the 3 functions were deleted from registry and the entry without a shim layer, along with the 6 rows in the known table (3 names each of doeff_adr and doeff_adr._registry). The macro defadr's expansion runs the registration of the deftests in :enforcement at import time as RegisterDeftest, and the verification of the generated test (test_<ID>_adr_contract) as AssertAdrContract, both under adr_registry() — like R22's defsemgrep it does not rely on the project's doeff_interpreter fixture, so defadr in outside repos works just by raising the doeff version.")
     (rule R31 "Where Hy macros outside doeff-hy live and the entry of doeff-hy's tools (the rest of R13 (c) — 19 rows in the known table) were decided without asking, in the same direction as R14, R15, R7, R10 and R16 (2026-10-30). The macro-exporting modules — the workflow package's macros module (13 workflow macros), doeff_events.macros (event-loop) and doeff_domain.macros (defdomain) — are, because Hy's require reads macros by module path, put at the boundary outside R5 as with R14 and R15, with no _ on the name — the 2 that step 1 had hidden as _macros with a shim module, the workflow package's and events', were returned to public modules. The inventory names of a macro-exporting module are only Hy macros. doeff-hy's CLI doeff-hy-check is kept, like R7's CLI commands. doeff_hy.pytest_plugin was deleted rather than allowed — contrary to the premise at the time of deciding (that install is the pytest plugin registration), the pytest11 plugin registration is held by doeff-hy-pytest (R10, moved on 2026-09-26), places reading this module were 0 in doeff and the repos in ~/repos, and install was an inner function of doeff_hy._ast_unparse. doeff_docker.compose's macro with-handlers (expanding a list of handlers into nested calls) shows users a way of applying handlers different from R16's official form h1(h2(p)), so it was deleted and its callers, 2 places in doeff-ml-nexus, rewritten to h1(h2(p)). The allowed names are put with reasons in the allowed table's groups 'allowed — Hy macro' (rows whose inventory form is a Hy macro name, and rows of public submodules exporting only Hy macros) and 'allowed — CLI command' (rows of console scripts and Rust executables), and the table's test checks the form per group (R12).")
     (rule R32 "doeff-adr's 4 functions that read and write the registry (register_adr, clear_registry, enforcement_ids, get_enforcement) became the effects RegisterAdr (register an ADR → AdrSpec), ClearRegistry (empty ADRs and checks → None), EnforcementIds (list of check ids → list[str]) and GetEnforcement (look up a check by id → EnforcementRef or SemgrepSpec), answered by the same handler of R20's factory adr_registry (2026-10-30 — put doeff-adr in scope and fix the remaining names in the same direction as R20-R22). Outside repos (agora-controllers, agora, custody and others) had no callers, so the 4 were deleted from registry and the entry without a shim layer, and the bodies put inside as registry._register_adr, _clear_registry, _enforcement_ids and _get_enforcement (all take the table as an argument, None = the process registry). The 8 rows in the known table (4 names each of doeff_adr and doeff_adr._registry) were removed too. A separate registry has the same form as R30's parameter enforcements: the ADR table is a copy of specs (created once when the factory is called, and RegisterAdr registers into it), and the check table is the given dict itself — passing the same dict as semgrep_enforcements(table=…) lets GetEnforcement and RegisterAdr's check ids read RegisterSemgrep's registrations. The 4 effects do not fall through from a separate registry to the outside (a missing id is KeyError), and ClearRegistry empties only the 2 tables of the separate registry. Defined failures of registering and looking up (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError) are thrown back with TransferThrow to where the effect was emitted. The macro defadr's expansion also runs ADR registration at import time as the effect RegisterAdr under adr_registry() (the same form as R30's RegisterDeftest). Tests that read and write only through effects are served by a handler installing a separate registry and do not need registry.isolated_registry. isolated_registry stays in registry as an inner tool used only by tests of where the macro expansion registers into the process registry at import time (the expansion installs its handler itself, so it cannot be swapped from outside — hidden when the submodule moves to _).")
     (rule R33 "doeff-adr (the package of executable-ADR macros and the pytest plugin) is put in the scope 'in rule scope', and R1 and R5 apply as for other packages (2026-10-30 — put it in scope). Public names are only effects and handlers (R20, R21, R22, R30, R32), R2 types, the CLI command doeff-adr (R7), the pytest11 plugin registration, and the macro-exporting module doeff_adr.macros (the same direction as R14 and R31). The 12 submodules were moved to _ (doeff 64ac67a2a), and after the 5 old-path callers in outside repos (agora-controllers and jev-proxy) were rewritten to the entry and doeff_adr.macros, the 12 shim modules were deleted (the former move tool finish). The rows of doeff-adr's outlier groups in the known table (hide the submodule with _ 12, move to an inner module 174) and the 3 boundary rows overlapping the entry's rows were removed, and the scope was revised to 'in rule scope'. The remaining boundary rows (types, CLI, pytest plugin registration, Hy macros) are moved by the card moving them to the allowed table. Removed from R13 (b).")
     (rule R34 "No child with the same name as a name the import root's entry (__init__) exports (a module, package or namespace dir directly below the root, or a shim module) is placed (2026-10-30). Importing the old path (<root>.<child>) makes the import system bind the parent package's attribute <root>.<child> to the child module — since the attribute exists, the entry's lazy __getattr__ is not called, an eagerly bound name is overwritten too, and callers taking the name from the entry later get a module instead of the function. Which wins is decided by import order. Actual case: doeff 42f897000 moved doeff_core_effects' child aiohttp_http_server to _ and left a shim module of the same name where the entry lazily exports the function aiohttp_http_server. agora-controllers' production foundation read the old path, so on 10-30 09:42-13:12 JST the record service crashed on every startup with TypeError (with_handlers: handler must be callable, got module), and clients could not connect to the screen. Shims of the same form were also in doeff_core_effects' scripted_http_server, an agentic package's opencode_handler and tmux_handler, and doeff_openrouter's structured_llm (which had a workaround swapping the package's class to discard the binding). The 2 in doeff_core_effects were deleted after their readers in outside repos (agora-controllers, moneta) were rewritten to the entry or the _ path, and the remaining 3 were deleted by commit 32ba0e517, which deleted the shims of 21 packages. A name the entry lazily reads from a package's attribute is likewise hidden if that package has a child of the same name (an agent package's agent was read from _effects, so importing the moved package's old path <root>.effects.agent turned it into a module — fixed to read from the defining module ._effects.agent). the former move tool's hide refuses without writing anything if a child's name equals a name the entry exports (names to add to the entry, names already in the entry, names the entry already binds) — rename the child or the entry name first. The entries of packages below the root (including _ packages) likewise place no child with the same name as a name they export — when the shim's finder answers the moved package's old path <root>.<old child>.<below> with the module at the moved destination, the import system binds the destination package's attribute <below> to the child module. At the time, that agent package's _effects entry did not export the function agent (same name as the child module agent — the root entry exported it from the defining module ._effects.agent. Moving the child to _ would have changed the effect classes' __module__ and broken the paths outside repos read (agora-controllers' foundation closure matching, semgrep rules), so the child was not moved), and the workflow package's _handlers' children agent_handler, exec_handler, git_handler, issue_handler, workspace_handler and _templates' children basic_pr, enforced_pr, multi_agent, reviewed_pr were moved to _ names (readers in outside repos were 0 — the 2026-10-30 count, the default branches of every repo in ~/repos).")
     (rule R35 "Functions converting an effect's answer to another measure (conversion functions) are allowed name by name (2026-10-30 — allow as conversion functions name by name, without changing callers). A function counted as a conversion meets these 3: (1) its return type is a value type — not an effect, handler or program type, and not Any, object or Callable, which say nothing of the content. (2) it is neither a generator nor a coroutine. (3) inside, it makes no effect, handler or program (reads no effect type or function building an effect, no handler value, builder or factory, no @do or defk function, Perform, WithHandler or handler control), and reads by name no clock, environment, file, process, randomness or terminal (now, time, monotonic, sleep, environ, getenv, open, read_text, run, Popen, socket, urlopen, random, uuid4, print and others — the inventory tool's _WORLD_NAMES). The inside is the function together with everything reached by following the same package's functions it reads. As with R18, the inventory's reading looks at the names inside as global values or attribute names and follows only the same package's functions, so things received as arguments, things read from module containers or instance attributes, and forms where another package's function touches the outside are invisible and read as conversions. Hence conversion functions are allowed name by name, and people read the inside of allowed names. Allowed names are put in the allowed table (tests/data/public_api_allowed.json) with the group 'allowed — conversion function' and a reason, and the inventory tool confirms the form of the name and turns red if it is not of conversion form (R12). Even if of conversion form, a function not in the allowed table remains an outlier. Names allowed now: doeff-time's epoch_ms_of (floors the time of GetTime's answer to epoch milliseconds for lining up with other processes' and records' stamps — the single owner of the rounding formula).")
     (rule R36 "The install that the .pth shipped by doeff-hy (the one line of doeff_hy_bytecode_guard.pth: import doeff_hy_bytecode_guard; doeff_hy_bytecode_guard.install()) calls on every start of the venv's Python is, like R10's pytest11 plugin registration, kept in the entry as a startup registration an outside reader (Python's site) reads by name, and put in the allowed table's group 'allowed — startup call' (2026-10-30 — a clause decided without asking as an extension of R10 and R14, which can be overturned later). Reason: the .pth is copied into the venv's site-packages by hatchling's force-include at install time, and even with an editable install the copy keeps its content as of installation (it is not rewritten when doeff is upgraded). If install were deleted from the entry or moved inside, an old venv's Python with the copy would raise AttributeError at the .pth line on every start, and the bytecode guard would not be installed (the outside startup callers are that .pth, doeff-cluster's tests/test_boot_wheel.hy writing the same one line, and the dotfiles check calling install). This is the only name outside R14's 'names other than macros move to inner modules'; the entry's other names (R2 types MacroRecord, HyMacroUseWarning) follow R14. Names in the group 'allowed — startup call' may be placed only when the inventory tool (startup_calls) reads the .pth shipped by the force-include of the package's pyproject.toml and they match a (module, name) called at startup (R12).")
     (rule R37 "Functions that assemble values describing a program's shape and neither emit effects nor have side effects (builders), and the types of the values they return, are allowed (2026-10-30 — add the clause, the inventory checks it). A function counted as a builder meets these 3: (1) its return type is a class defined by the function's package (the name of its import root) and shown by that package (the inventory has a bare class row) — not an effect, handler, program or exception type. (2) it is neither a generator nor a coroutine (emits no effects). (3) inside, it makes no effect, handler or program, and reads by name no clock, environment, file, process, randomness or terminal (the same inventory tool's _WORLD_NAMES as R35). The inside is the function together with everything reached by following the same package's functions it reads. A class counted as a type is one returned by some builder placed in the same group of the allowed table. As with R18 and R35, the inventory's reading looks at the names inside as global values or attribute names and follows only the same package's functions, so the form calling a callable received as an argument (the body parallel_for receives), inside class construction (dataclass default_factory — workspace!'s WorkspaceSpec takes its occurrence number from a module counter, and the loading tool resets the counter per module), and forms where another package's function touches the outside are invisible and read as builders. Hence builders are allowed name by name, and people read the inside of allowed names. Allowed names are put in the allowed table (tests/data/public_api_allowed.json) with the group 'allowed — program-shape builders and types' and an answer id, and the inventory tool confirms the form of the name and turns red if it is not of builder form, or if a type is not a class returned by the group's builders (R12). Even if of builder form, a function not in the allowed table remains an outlier. Names allowed now: doeff-stream's 3 builders (merge, subscribe, timers) and the 2 types they return (Inputs, Source). The 66 rows (47 names — the workflow package's 28 builders and 19 types, including rows of modules before the names moved to the entry) of the former known-table group 'boundary — workflow DSL words' were moved, and names not fitting the form were 0; they left the allowed table when that package was moved out of this repository.")]
  :laws
    [(law subpackage-shows-only-effects-and-handlers
       :statement "for_all package p under packages/ with scope(p) = in rule scope: outside(p) = {n in public_names(p) | kind(n) not in {effect, handler}} = known(p) + allowed(p) with known(p) and allowed(p) disjoint, listed_names(table) only shrinks from its first version, and the known table holds no allowed group"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "The root of an agent package exports launch_session, and the tutorial uses it to start a session by hand (the form that started this ADR)")
          (counterexample "A new helper function is added to a subpackage's root and passes without being written in the known table or in added names")
          (counterexample "An outlier is fixed but its name is left in the known table, so the table does not shrink")
          (counterexample "A new name is written into the known table's 'names' groups so that an added outlier passes")
          (counterexample "A package with a Rust extension is not installed in the environment, the inventory reads it from the text, and it passes without counting names the entry exports from the extension (the former Indexer of doeff-indexer)")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_an_added_outlier_turns_red_and_removing_it_turns_green"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_fixed_name_left_in_the_table_turns_red"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_native_package_in_scope_that_the_inventory_cannot_import_turns_red"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_known_table_only_shrinks_from_its_first_version"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_name_put_into_the_listed_groups_turns_red"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-an-added-outlier-turns-the-guard-red"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-the-known-table-is-well-formed-and-only-shrinks"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-26) — 39 per-package cases, red and green of a throwaway package, the table's form, and only-shrinks. This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law a-handler-is-a-marked-value-and-a-factory-returns-one
       :statement "handler(h) <=> marked(h) (h: ProgramHandler); factory(f) <=> for the arguments f needs at least (none when every parameter has a default): marked(f()) or f() is a list or tuple of marked values; counted_as_factory(f) and callable_without_arguments(f) and not factory(f) => outside(f); takes(f, Program) and returns(f, Program) and not marked(f) => not handler(f); class c named *Handler => not handler(c)"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Allowing an unmarked function that takes a program and returns a program, like maybe(p) before 2026-10-29, by counting it as a handler because its shape is program -> program")
          (counterexample "A function whose return type says ProgramHandler returns, when called, an unmarked function that passes the program through, yet is counted as a factory returning a handler by its return type alone and passes outside the table")
          (counterexample "Making public a class whose name ends with Handler by counting it as a handler because it receives effects in a method")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_names_are_typed_by_marker_return_type_and_return_shape"
                     "tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_a_factory_says_whether_its_call_was_checked"
                     "tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_a_factory_is_called_with_home_and_cwd_in_a_scratch_dir"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_factory_whose_call_is_not_a_handler_turns_red"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-a-factory-whose-call-is-not-a-handler-turns-the-guard-red"]
       :wiring "Partly wired (2026-10-29) — the inventory counts handlers by the marker, treats unmarked classes with Handler names as unclassified, and reads (effect, k) functions as handlers. Functions read as factories by return type or return shape are called with minimal arguments (none, or a placeholder for each parameter without a default) (with HOME, the XDG dirs and cwd pointed at a temporary dir) to check the marker, and returning an unmarked value counts as the outlier 'function not returning a handler', so it is red unless in the known table. Factories that raise when called stay as read from the return type (the exception is written in the row's reason — 21 of 225 in the 2026-10-29 inventory). Write destinations a module fixed at import time are not pointed at the temporary dir. This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law the-public-names-come-from-the-import-root
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all public submodule m of p: m's path has a part starting with _ — enforced as: public_submodules(p) = known_submodules(p), known_submodules only shrinks from the version that added them, and a name n moved from a submodule s to the import root r is the same row as (s, n) when s no longer shows n or shows the same definition"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Having effects imported from a public submodule <root>.effects, showing the same name through 2 interfaces, the root and a submodule")
          (counterexample "Adding a new submodule without _ to a subpackage and passing without writing it in the known table or added names because it exports only effects")
          (counterexample "Moving an R2 type from a submodule to the entry is read as a change of the table's key (the module exporting the name) and turns red, leaving as the only fixes writing into the table's names groups or into added names")
          (counterexample "Adding to the entry a different definition with the same name as one a submodule still exports, and passing as a name moved to the entry")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_an_added_public_submodule_turns_red_and_hiding_it_turns_green"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_moving_names_from_a_submodule_to_the_root_keeps_the_table_green"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_different_definition_with_a_known_name_at_the_root_turns_red"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_marked_shim_keeps_the_modules_below_a_moved_package_at_their_old_names"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_known_table_only_shrinks_from_its_first_version"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_submodule_row_put_into_the_table_after_it_had_submodule_rows_turns_red"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-a-public-submodule-turns-the-guard-red-and-a-moved-name-does-not"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-28) — the inventory counts a submodule not starting with _ as an outlier of 1 row (the field for the module exporting the name is (submodule)), the known table lists the current 731 public submodule rows and only shrinks from the version that added submodule rows. A row whose name moved from a submodule to the entry is read as the same row without rewriting the table's module (while the submodule still exports the same name, only when the definition's place is the same; after the old interface is closed, by name and root only). A shim module with the marker # moved-submodule-shim: <old name> -> <new name> on its first line (placed by the former move tool) also counts the modules under the destination package by their old names, so stage 1 is green without rewriting the table. Rows of hidden submodules are removed from the table. This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law an-allowed-program-helper-only-emits-effects
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — program helper): returns_program(n) and for_all body b in bodies(n): not reads(b, handler_control) and not reads(b, handler_installer) — bodies(n) = n and the functions of p that n reads, followed through; handler_control = {Resume, ResumeThrow, Transfer, TransferThrow, Pass}; handler_installer = WithHandler or a marked value or a handler builder or a factory; an allowed group is a group whose names the inventory checks; and every row of the allowed table names its decision (a card, request or remark id)"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Passing a function that takes a program, applies a handler value and returns it (maybe's current form) by putting it in the helper group because it returns a program")
          (counterexample "Passing a function in the helper group where another function of the same package installs a handler with WithHandler, by reading only inside the group's name")
          (counterexample "Counting as a helper a function that emits Resume inside to answer an effect")
          (counterexample "Passing a function that does not return a program (one returning a spec value, like check) by putting it in the helper group")
          (counterexample "Creating in the table an allowed group whose form is not checked (e.g. allowed — convenience functions) and moving outlier names there to pass")
          (counterexample "Writing a function shown later in added names as a helper, passing a function that installs handlers without the group's check")
          (counterexample "Adding a row without a reason to the allowed table, passing a name nobody allowed")
          (counterexample "Leaving allowed groups in the known table, so the known table never becomes empty even after all outliers and boundaries are fixed")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_a_program_helper_can_be_put_into_the_helper_group"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_an_allowed_group_without_a_check_is_not_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_an_allowed_row_without_a_reason_is_not_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_allowed_table_is_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_what_the_helper_reading_does_not_see"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_names_are_typed_by_marker_return_type_and_return_shape"
                     "tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_a_program_function_says_whether_it_is_a_helper"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-program-helper-can-be-allowed-as-one"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-29) — the inventory reads from the code object the names inside a function returning a program (and inside the same package's functions it reads); if it reads handler control or something installing handlers, it counts the form 'function returning a program that installs handlers' or 'function returning a program that answers effects', otherwise the form 'program helper' (the kind is other in every case). The table's test turns red if a name in the allowed table's group 'allowed — program helper' is not of helper form; only groups whose form is checked may be put in the allowed table, and rows have reasons (on 2026-10-30 allowed things were split from the known table into the allowed table tests/data/public_api_allowed.json — the former move tool also reads both tables). Forms the reading does not see (a handler received as an argument, another package's function installing it) are fixed in the inventory's test as the current limit (structural review 2026-10-29 finding). This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law an-allowed-conversion-makes-nothing-and-reads-no-world
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — conversion function): value_return_type(n) and not generator(n) and not coroutine(n) and for_all body b in bodies(n): not reads(b, maker) and not reads(b, world) — bodies(n) = n and the functions of p that n reads, followed through; maker = an effect type or a function returning one, a marked handler or a handler builder or a factory, a @do or defk function, Perform, WithHandler or handler control; world = a name of _WORLD_NAMES (a clock, the environment, a file, a process, randomness or the console)"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Passing a function that reads the current time and returns epoch milliseconds (reads datetime.now) by putting it in the conversion group because it returns an integer")
          (counterexample "Passing a function in the conversion group where another function of the same package reads time.time, by reading only inside the group's name")
          (counterexample "Counting as a conversion a function that builds an effect value inside, because its return type is an integer")
          (counterexample "Passing a function returning a program or a factory returning a handler by putting it in the conversion group")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_a_conversion_can_be_put_into_the_conversion_group"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_allowed_table_is_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_names_are_typed_by_marker_return_type_and_return_shape"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-conversion-can-be-allowed-as-one"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30) — the inventory reads from the code object the names inside a function whose return type is a value type (and inside the same package's functions it reads); if it reads something making an effect, handler or program or a name of _WORLD_NAMES, or is a generator or coroutine, it counts the form 'function' (at the end of the reason, which function reads which name), otherwise the form 'conversion function' (the kind is other in both). The table's test turns red if a name in the allowed table's group 'allowed — conversion function' is not of conversion form. The current row is the one doeff-time epoch_ms_of")
     (law an-allowed-macro-or-command-has-that-form
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — Hy macro): form(m, n) = Hy macro or (m = (submodule) and names(n) != {} and for_all k in names(n): form(n, k) = Hy macro); for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — CLI command): form(m, n) in {console script, Rust executable} and n in ADR_COMMANDS(p) — ADR_COMMANDS(p) = the commands of p the test lists with the rule that keeps each, and that rule's text names the command"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Step 1 hides the macro-exporting module as _macros, users' (require <package>.macros …) depends on the shim module, and when step 3 deletes the shim module the macros have no public home (the workflow package's macros module and doeff_events.macros before R31)")
          (counterexample "Adding a non-macro function to a macro-exporting module and passing the function shown from the public module while that submodule's row stays in the macro group")
          (counterexample "Passing a non-macro function or a console script by putting it in the macro group")
          (counterexample "Passing a macro or a function by putting it in the CLI command group")
          (counterexample "Passing a new console script that no ADR clause names by putting it in the CLI command group because its form is a console script (a resident startup interface like doeff-sessionhost, which R8 deleted, comes back without a decision)")
          (counterexample "Writing a new command into the command-and-clause table and passing without revising the clause text")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_a_macro_or_a_command_can_be_put_into_the_macro_and_command_groups"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_command_the_adr_does_not_name_turns_red"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_every_command_the_guard_allows_is_named_by_its_rule"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_allowed_table_is_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-macro-or-a-command-can-be-allowed-as-one"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30) — the table's test turns a row of the allowed table's group 'allowed — Hy macro' green only when its inventory form is a Hy macro, or it is a row of a public submodule whose inventory names are all Hy macros, and a row of the group 'allowed — CLI command' green only when it is a console script or Rust executable row that ADR_COMMANDS lists as a command of that package (the current rows are 10 commands of 9 packages). This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law an-allowed-effect-value-type-is-written-in-a-field-of-an-effect-its-package-shows
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — effect value type): value(m, n) in value_types(p) — value_types(p) = the classes and named type aliases written in the field annotations (dataclass fields, or the parameters of an __init__ written in Python), type arguments included, of the effect classes p shows, followed through the fields of the classes p defines"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Passing a class that appears in no effect's field (a client, a settings reader, an inner helper class) by putting it in the value type group because it is a class")
          (counterexample "Looking at field annotations only by the outer type (list, Optional, Union), missing the Payload in list[Payload] among the type arguments, and leaving the value type as an outlier")
          (counterexample "Reading a class of another package with the same name as a value type by looking it up by name (not comparing the identical value)")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_an_effect_value_type_can_be_put_into_the_value_type_group"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_allowed_table_is_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-only-an-effect-value-type-can-be-allowed-as-one"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30) — per package, the inventory collects types from the field annotations of the effect classes it shows (annotation text is resolved with the names of the effect's module), also follows the fields of the value types the package defines, and writes into value_types the shown names whose value is the same as such a type (with the field's place <effect>.<field>). The table's test turns a row of the allowed table's group 'allowed — effect value type' green only when the name is in value_types. The current rows are 323 rows (108 names). This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law an-allowed-setting-type-is-a-type-its-package-defines-and-a-public-handler-takes
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — handler setting type): value(m, n) in settings(p) and defined_by(n, p) — settings(p) = the classes and named type aliases written in the parameter annotations, type arguments included, of the handlers, handler classes (their __init__) and factories p shows, followed through the fields of the classes p defines; defined_by(n, p) = the place the inventory gives for n's definition is a file under p's dir or a module under one of p's import roots"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Passing a class that appears in no handler or factory parameter (an inner runtime class, an effect value type) by putting it in the setting type group because it is an R2 type")
          (counterexample "Re-exporting a standard library class (pathlib's Path and the like) through a submodule's __all__, and passing it in the setting type group because it appears in a factory parameter")
          (counterexample "Looking at factory parameter annotations only by the outer type (Sequence, Optional) or not reading string annotations, leaving a setting type like the ScriptedCommand in Sequence[ScriptedCommand] as an outlier")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_a_type_a_handler_takes_can_be_put_into_the_setting_group"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_allowed_table_is_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-type-a-handler-takes-can-be-allowed-as-a-setting-type"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30) — per package, the inventory collects types from the parameter annotations of the handlers, handler classes and factories it shows (annotation text is resolved with the names of the function's module), also follows the fields of the setting types the package defines, and writes into settings the shown names whose value is the same as such a type (with the parameter's place <handler>(<parameter>) or the field's place <class>.<field>). The table's test turns a row of the allowed table's group 'allowed — handler setting type' green only when the name is in settings and its definition's place is under that package. The current rows are 115 rows (65 names). This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law an-allowed-plugin-registration-or-startup-call-is-what-the-package-installs
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — pytest plugin registration): form(m, n) = entry point(pytest11) or m in plugin_modules(p); for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — startup call): (m, n) in startup_calls(p) — plugin_modules(p) = the modules p's pyproject.toml names as pytest11 entry points; startup_calls(p) = the (module, name) of each module.name(...) call in a line starting with import of a .pth file p's force-include installs"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Passing functions outside the plugin module (classes and helpers inside the measurement) by putting them in the plugin group because they carry the plugin package's name")
          (counterexample "Passing an entry function the startup .pth does not call by putting it in the startup call group")
          (counterexample "Leaving install in the startup call group and passing after the .pth is no longer shipped")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_a_plugin_registration_or_a_startup_call_can_be_put_into_their_groups"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_allowed_table_is_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-plugin-registration-or-a-startup-call-can-be-allowed-as-one"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30) — the inventory tool's plugin_modules reads the modules the package's pytest11 entry points load, and startup_calls reads the calls in the lines starting with import of the force-include .pth. The table's test turns a row of the allowed table's group 'allowed — pytest plugin registration' green only when it is a pytest11 entry point row or a name the plugin module exports, and a row of the group 'allowed — startup call' green only when it is a (module, name) the .pth calls. The current rows are the pytest11 registrations of doeff-adr and doeff-hy-pytest, doeff-hy-pytest's 2 declarations (declare_step_budget, declare_process_tree_cpu), and doeff-hy's install. This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law an-allowed-builder-builds-a-shape-value-its-package-shows
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — program-shape builders and types): (builder(n) and returns(n) in shown_classes(p)) or (plain_class(n) and n in {returns(b) | b in allowed(p, same group), builder(b)}) — builder(n) = conversion(n) (R35: not generator, not coroutine, no maker and no world read in bodies(n)) and n's return type is a class defined under n's own top-level package; returns(n) = that class (its name and place); shown_classes(p) = the plain classes p's inventory shows"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Passing a function that yields an effect inside by putting it in the builder group because its return type is a package type")
          (counterexample "Passing a function in the builder group where another function of the same package reads os.environ or a file, by reading only inside the group's name")
          (counterexample "Counting as a builder a function returning an unshown class with an _, because it returns a package class — users cannot read the returned value's type by name")
          (counterexample "Passing a conversion function returning int or dict by putting it in the builder group because it assembles a value")
          (counterexample "Passing a class that none of the group's builders return by putting it in the builder group because it is in the type group")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_a_builder_and_the_class_it_returns_can_be_put_into_the_builder_group"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_allowed_table_is_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_names_are_typed_by_marker_return_type_and_return_shape"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-builder-and-its-class-can-be-allowed-as-one"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30) — among functions of conversion form (R35), the inventory counts as the form 'builder' those whose return type is a class defined by the function's package, and writes that class's name and definition place in the row's field returns (the kind is other). The table's test turns a row of the allowed table's group 'allowed — program-shape builders and types' green only when its form is a builder and the package shows the returns class, or it is a plain class returned by some builder of the group. The current rows are doeff-stream's 5 rows (3 builder names, 2 type names). This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law an-allowed-exception-type-is-an-exception-class-its-package-defines
       :statement "for_all package p in scope: for_all (m, n) in allowed(p, allowed — exception type): form(m, n) = exception class (a subclass of BaseException) and defined_by(n, p) — defined_by(n, p) = the place the inventory gives for n's definition is a file under p's dir or a module under one of p's import roots"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Passing a class that is not an exception (an effect result type or a handler setting type) by putting it in the exception group because it is an R2 type")
          (counterexample "Re-exporting an exception of the standard library or another package (json's JSONDecodeError and the like) through a submodule's __all__, and passing it in the exception group because it is a subclass of BaseException")
          (counterexample "Checking the exception group by the name's suffix (ending with Error), passing functions and constants whose names end with Error")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_an_exception_class_of_its_package_can_be_put_into_the_exception_group"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_allowed_table_is_well_formed"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_public_api_outside_effects_and_handlers_is_in_the_known_table"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-only-an-exception-class-of-its-package-can-be-allowed-as-one"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30) — the inventory reads subclasses of BaseException as the form 'exception class' and writes the definition's place in the row (inside the repo, file and line; outside the repo, the module name). The table's test turns a row of the allowed table's group 'allowed — exception type' green only when its form is an exception class and its definition's place is under that package's dir or a module under its import root (rows of names that do not fit name the form, reason and definition place). The current rows are 247 rows (129 names) of 16 packages. This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law a-doeff-part-is-imported-only-by-doeff
       :statement "for_all part q in DOEFF_PARTS (doeff-vm -> doeff_vm): q not in package_dirs(inventory) and q not in table; for_all file f under packages/ (outside q's dir), examples/ or docs/adr/: not imports(f, root(q)) — imports(f, r) = f's import or from statement, a module of Hy's import or require form, or a module-name string passed to import_module, importorskip, __import__ or find_spec, whose root is r"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "After doeff-vm is put outside the inventory's scope, a doeff subpackage reads the VM directly with from doeff_vm import K, and a name the inventory does not count becomes an interface users use")
          (counterexample "Reading by name string, as in pytest.importorskip(\"doeff_vm\") or importlib.import_module(\"doeff_vm\"), slipping past a check that looks only at import statements (the form of doeff-effect-analyzer's check before R11)")
          (counterexample "Listing modules in one form, as in Hy's (import pytest doeff_vm [Pass]), slipping past a check that looks only at the module at the head of the form")
          (counterexample "Leaving doeff-vm's entry in the known table after doeff-vm is removed from the inventory")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_only_doeff_imports_its_parts"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_doeff_part_imported_outside_doeff_turns_red"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_the_known_table_has_one_well_formed_entry_per_package_dir"
                     "tests/test_public_api_inventory.py::test_every_package_dir_is_listed"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-a-doeff-part-imported-outside-doeff-turns-the-guard-red"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30) — the inventory tool does not count the dirs of DOEFF_PARTS as packages, and the table-form check turns entries outside the inventory's packages red as 'in the table but not under packages/'. The table's test reads files outside doeff — Python by syntax tree, Hy by tokens — and names by file, line and module the places reading a part's import root. Repos outside doeff are invisible — the rewrite and per-repo watching are held by another card. The semgrep rule doeff-vm-imported-only-by-doeff stops import statements of the same places at writing time, and this test also sees the forms reading by name string and Hy's listed forms. This file's deftests only call those functions")
     (law an-entry-name-is-not-a-submodule-name
       :statement "for_all package p under packages/: for_all import root r of p: for_all package q in {r} + the packages below r (packages whose names start with _ included): children(q) ∩ bound(entry(q)) = {} — children(q) = the names of the module files, package dirs and namespace dirs right below q whose names do not start with _ (moved-submodule shims included); bound(entry(q)) = the public names q/__init__.py binds at top level (def, class, assignment, _LAZY_EXPORTS key, from-import that does not bind the child module itself); and in a fresh process, after importing r, then every package below r, then every public module of p (each old path a shim stands for and the old names below a moved package included) one by one: for_all package q and public name n of dir(q) not bound to a module when q was first imported: getattr(q, n) is not a module"
       :counterexamples
         [(counterexample "Moving the child aiohttp_http_server to _aiohttp_http_server and leaving a shim module of the same name where the entry lazily exports the function aiohttp_http_server (doeff 42f897000 — the record service reading the old path crashed on every startup)")
          (counterexample "The entry eagerly binds the function tmux_handler from _tmux_handler and leaves a shim module of the same name — after importing the old path, the entry's name is a module")
          (counterexample "A shim module swaps the package's class to discard the module binding, hiding the import order from the fresh-process check (the former hide workaround — the text matching turns it red)")
          (counterexample "The entry's lazy table reads a name from a package's attribute and that package has a child of the same name — importing the moved package's old path overwrites the package's attribute with the child module (the former agent of an agent package)")
          (counterexample "hide places a shim module on a child of the same name without looking at the names it adds to the entry")
          (counterexample "The entry of a package below the root binds a name from a child of the same name — importing the moved package's old path <root>.<old child>.<below> overwrites the destination package's attribute <below> with the child module (the former agent of an agent package's _effects and its old path <root>.effects.agent, and the workflow package's _handlers' agent_handler and 4 others and _templates' basic_pr and 3 others)")]
       :enforced-by ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_no_submodule_is_named_like_a_name_its_entry_shows"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_a_submodule_named_like_an_entry_name_turns_red_and_removing_it_turns_green"
                     "tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py::test_an_inner_package_entry_name_like_its_child_turns_red_and_moving_the_child_turns_green"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-a-submodule-named-like-an-entry-name-turns-the-guard-red"
                     "docs/adr/defadr_doeff_api_001_subpackages_show_effects_and_handlers.hy::test-adr-doe-api-001-an-inner-package-entry-name-like-its-child-turns-the-guard-red"]
       :wiring "Wired (2026-10-30 — later extended to packages below the root) — the table's test checks each package in 2 ways: text matching (the syntax tree of the entry files of the root and of packages below the root (including _ packages) against the names of the children right below that package — independent of import order), and a fresh-process check (import the root, import the packages below the root one by one and note the entry names, import the public modules and old paths one by one, and if a noted entry name turned into a module, name after which import). The test runs neither in the merge queue nor in the commit hook; it runs in the daily full verification after merge (stage root). hide goes in the same direction as this check and refuses before moving a child of the same name")]
  :enforcement
    [(deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-an-added-outlier-turns-the-guard-red
       ;; R12: enforcement is held by the table's test, and this only calls its functions — adding one function outside effects and handlers
       ;; to a throwaway package is red, removing it is green, leaving a fixed name in the table is red, and a package in rule scope with a Rust extension that cannot be read by import is red (R27).
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-an-added-outlier-turns-red-and-removing-it-turns-green (/ (Path scratch) "added"))
         (guard.test-a-fixed-name-left-in-the-table-turns-red (/ (Path scratch) "fixed"))
         (guard.test-a-native-package-in-scope-that-the-inventory-cannot-import-turns-red (/ (Path scratch) "native"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-the-known-table-is-well-formed-and-only-shrinks
       ;; R12: the known table holds one entry per dir under packages/, holds no allowed groups, and its names groups only shrink from the version of the commit that added the table.
       ;; Rows of the allowed table have a group and a reason (an ADR clause or one English sentence) (calls functions of the same test).
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (guard.test-the-known-table-has-one-well-formed-entry-per-package-dir)
       (guard.test-the-allowed-table-is-well-formed)
       (guard.test-the-known-table-only-shrinks-from-its-first-version)
       (guard.test-a-name-put-into-the-listed-groups-turns-red)
       (guard.test-a-submodule-row-put-into-the-table-after-it-had-submodule-rows-turns-red))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-a-public-submodule-turns-the-guard-red-and-a-moved-name-does-not
       ;; R5, R12: only calls functions of the same test — adding a submodule without _ to a throwaway package is red and hiding it is green; moving names from a submodule
       ;; to the entry is green without rewriting the table (both while a shim module remains and after it is deleted); adding a different definition with the same name to the entry is red.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-an-added-public-submodule-turns-red-and-hiding-it-turns-green (/ (Path scratch) "submodule"))
         (guard.test-moving-names-from-a-submodule-to-the-root-keeps-the-table-green (/ (Path scratch) "moved") "lazy" "alias")
         (guard.test-a-marked-shim-keeps-the-modules-below-a-moved-package-at-their-old-names (/ (Path scratch) "marked"))
         (guard.test-a-different-definition-with-a-known-name-at-the-root-turns-red (/ (Path scratch) "different"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-a-factory-whose-call-is-not-a-handler-turns-the-guard-red
       ;; R3, R12: only calls functions of the same test — a name in a throwaway package read as a factory by its return type that returns an unmarked value when called is red,
       ;; and fixing it to return a handler is green.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-a-factory-whose-call-is-not-a-handler-turns-red (/ (Path scratch) "factory"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-program-helper-can-be-allowed-as-one
       ;; R18, R12: only calls functions of the same test — a program function of a throwaway package that only emits effects is green when put in the allowed table's group 'allowed —
       ;; program helper'. Putting a function that installs handlers, answers effects or does not return a program is red, and leaving a deleted name is red. An allowed group whose form
       ;; is not checked, allowed groups in the known table, and rows without a reason are red by the table-form check.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-only-a-program-helper-can-be-put-into-the-helper-group (/ (Path scratch) "helper")))
       (guard.test-an-allowed-group-without-a-check-is-not-well-formed)
       (guard.test-an-allowed-row-without-a-reason-is-not-well-formed))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-macro-or-a-command-can-be-allowed-as-one
       ;; R31, R12: only calls functions of the same test — a throwaway package's Hy macros and submodules exporting only macros are green in the group 'allowed — Hy
       ;; macro', and console scripts in the group 'allowed — CLI command'. Putting a submodule also exporting non-macro names, a function or a console script
       ;; in the macro group, or a macro or a function in the CLI command group, is red. Putting a console script no ADR clause names in the CLI command group
       ;; is red, and every command in the command-and-clause table is named by its clause text.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-only-a-macro-or-a-command-can-be-put-into-the-macro-and-command-groups (/ (Path scratch) "macro"))
         (guard.test-a-command-the-adr-does-not-name-turns-red (/ (Path scratch) "command")))
       (guard.test-every-command-the-guard-allows-is-named-by-its-rule))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-plugin-registration-or-a-startup-call-can-be-allowed-as-one
       ;; R10, R33, R36, R12: only calls functions of the same test — a throwaway package's pytest11 registration, plugin declarations, and the install the .pth
       ;; calls are green in their respective groups. Putting a name outside the plugin module in the plugin group, or a name the .pth does not call in the startup call group, is red,
       ;; and install is red too if the .pth is not shipped.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-only-a-plugin-registration-or-a-startup-call-can-be-put-into-their-groups (/ (Path scratch) "plugin"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-conversion-can-be-allowed-as-one
       ;; R35, R12: only calls functions of the same test — a throwaway package's function that only takes values and returns values is green in the group 'allowed — conversion function'.
       ;; Putting a function whose inner same-package function reads the clock, that reads the clock through an attribute, or that builds an effect is red.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-only-a-conversion-can-be-put-into-the-conversion-group (/ (Path scratch) "conversion"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-only-an-exception-class-of-its-package-can-be-allowed-as-one
       ;; R2, R12: only calls functions of the same test — an exception class defined by a throwaway package is green in the group 'allowed — exception type'.
       ;; Putting a class that is not an exception, or a name re-exporting a standard library exception through a submodule's __all__, is red.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-only-an-exception-class-of-its-package-can-be-put-into-the-exception-group (/ (Path scratch) "exception"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-builder-and-its-class-can-be-allowed-as-one
       ;; R37, R12: only calls functions of the same test — a throwaway package's builder that only returns a value of a shown class, and that class, are green in the group
       ;; 'allowed — program-shape builders and types'. Putting a function that emits or builds effects, whose inner function reads the environment or reads a file,
       ;; a function returning an unshown class, a function returning something other than a value type, or a class no builder of the group returns, is red.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-only-a-builder-and-the-class-it-returns-can-be-put-into-the-builder-group (/ (Path scratch) "builder"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-only-an-effect-value-type-can-be-allowed-as-one
       ;; R2, R12: only calls functions of the same test — classes appearing in a throwaway package's effect field type arguments, __init__ parameters, and value type fields
       ;; are green in the group 'allowed — effect value type'. Putting a class that appears in no effect's field is red.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-only-an-effect-value-type-can-be-put-into-the-value-type-group (/ (Path scratch) "value-type"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-only-a-type-a-handler-takes-can-be-allowed-as-a-setting-type
       ;; R2, R12: only calls functions of the same test — types appearing in a throwaway package's factory parameters (string annotations), inside type arguments, and in setting type fields
       ;; are green in the group 'allowed — handler setting type'. Putting a class or function appearing in no parameter, or a name re-exporting a standard library class, is red.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-only-a-type-a-handler-takes-can-be-put-into-the-setting-group (/ (Path scratch) "setting"))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-a-doeff-part-imported-outside-doeff-turns-the-guard-red
       ;; R11, R12: only calls functions of the same test — in a throwaway repo, a file outside doeff importing an internal part of doeff (doeff_vm) is
       ;; red (naming the lines of import statements, Hy forms and name strings), and rewriting it to read from doeff's root and doeff._vm is green. Imports by the part itself, doeff's root
       ;; package and doeff's checks are not counted.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-a-doeff-part-imported-outside-doeff-turns-red (Path scratch))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-a-submodule-named-like-an-entry-name-turns-the-guard-red
       ;; R34, R12: only calls functions of the same test — placing shim modules with the same names where a throwaway package's entry exports the functions serve (lazy) and tool (eager)
       ;; makes both the text matching and the fresh-process check red (after importing the old path, the entry's name is a module),
       ;; and deleting the 2 shims is green.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-a-submodule-named-like-an-entry-name-turns-red-and-removing-it-turns-green (Path scratch))))
     (deftest test-adr-doe-api-001-an-inner-package-entry-name-like-its-child-turns-the-guard-red
       ;; R34, R12: only calls functions of the same test — when the entry of the package _inner below a throwaway package's root binds the function tool from the child tool of the same name,
       ;; and a shim answers the moved package's old path inner, both the text matching and the fresh-process check are red (after importing the old
       ;; path inner.tool, _inner's name tool is a module), and moving the child to _tool is green.
       (<- guard ModuleType (guard-module))
       (with [scratch (tempfile.TemporaryDirectory)]
         (guard.test-an-inner-package-entry-name-like-its-child-turns-red-and-moving-the-child-turns-green (Path scratch))))]
  :plans ["tests/test_public_api_known_violations.py, tests/data/public_api_known_violations.json"
          "tools/public_api_inventory.py"
          "Cards of phases 2-4 of the initiative doeff-subpackages-effects-only (fixes — the agent package, CLI commands, doeff-vm, doeff-hy macros, writer_log, __version__, class handlers, submodules)"
          "After the R11 and R13 answers come: a card aligning the known table's scope and groups to the answers, and a card adding clauses to this ADR (the maintainer cuts them)"
          "R11: a card removing doeff-vm from the inventory and the known table as an internal part of doeff and having the table's test watch imports in files outside doeff (done). A card rewriting import doeff_vm in repos outside doeff to doeff's root and placing a watch per repo"
          "R6: a card reshaping maybe, result, on_raise and absent_as as factories returning handlers, a card rewriting callers in outside repos, and a card deleting the 4 old forms and removing the 4 names from the known table — all 3 done. A card making scheduled a marked handler value instead of a factory and emptying the known table's group 'outlier — reshape as a factory returning a handler' (done; the previously added factory form and rewriting tool were deleted). A card returning maybe and result to handler values"
          "R16: a card rewriting WithHandler in user-facing docs and examples to h1(h2(p)) (the maintainer cuts it)"
          "R18, R13 (d), R25: the question whether to allow the helper-shape program functions of the outlier groups, and a card moving the group of 155 names per the answer — both done. A card re-exporting from the entry the 36 names step 1 already left under _, and a card fixing the 4 pairs of same names colliding at the entry (the maintainer cuts them)"
          "R12, R18: a card splitting allowed things from the known table into the allowed table tests/data/public_api_allowed.json, making the known table able to become empty — done. A card turning R2's exception, effect value, effect result and handler setting types and the answered CLI, macros and plugins into allowing groups whose form the inventory checks and moving them to the allowed table (exception types, effect value types, handler setting types, CLI, plugins and startup calls are done. The plugin parts doeff-adr's entry exports, DEFAULT_FILE_PATTERNS and DoeffAdrHyFile, are not named by a clause and remain in the known table's group 'boundary — pytest plugin registration'). A card, per package, for the 158 names of the group 'outlier — type the effect field or move it inside', either typing the effect field and moving them to the allowed table or moving them inside (the maintainer cuts it)"
          "R37, R13 (c): a card adding to the inventory's check an allowing group for program-shape builders and their types, and moving the 66 rows of the workflow package's DSL words to the allowed table (done)"])
